// Made by scripts/build-help.mjs from docs/guides and docs/runbooks. Don't edit this by hand.
export const HELP_CONTENT: Record<string, string> = {
  "admin-assessments": "# Admin: assessments and competencies\n\n## How it fits together\n\n- A **competency** is a skill or piece of equipment someone has shown they can handle.\n- A **serving role** lists the competencies it needs.\n- A **practical assessment** is how someone earns a competency.\n- **Approved to Serve** follows automatically once someone holds all of a role's competencies.\n\nThe observer records what they saw. The system decides the result.\n\n## Assessments\n\n**Waiting for assessment** lists volunteers who asked to be assessed. Choose **Assess now**. To record one nobody requested, use **Record an assessment**, and choose the practical and the person you watched. The list shows the department's members and anyone else who has started training there.\n\n1. Watch them work.\n2. Tick what you saw.\n3. Add feedback. It's worth writing, especially for a \"Not yet\".\n\nThe Academy works out the result from the leader's pass rule. You don't pick the outcome. A \"Not yet\" has no waiting period: they can ask again when they're ready.\n\n**Recent assessments** keeps each one with who observed it, what they saw, and the result. If one was recorded by mistake, **Withdraw this assessment**, with a reason. It stays on record, marked as withdrawn.\n\n## Competencies and roles\n\n**Competencies:** add the skills and equipment people need to show they can handle. Choose **Edit or archive** to change one. Each can have an expiry, if a skill needs refreshing.\n\n**Serving roles:** add the roles people serve in, such as \"Camera 2 operator\", then choose what each one needs.\n\n**People:** competencies appear here when someone passes a practical. If there's a reason to stop relying on one, choose **Suspend**, with a reason. You can restore it later. They're told the skill is paused, but not your reason.\n\n## Serving teams\n\nTeams are covered in [Scheduling](admin-scheduling.md). Passing an assessment never adds anyone to a team, and \"Trained\" or \"Still learning\" shows beside each name but doesn't gate anything.\n",
  "admin-checklists": "# Admin: checklists\n\nThese pages set up what each position does before and after a service. For how the team uses them, see [Checklist](checklist.md).\n\n## Task lists\n\nA **task list** is a full set of department checklists. New services use the **Default** list, and keep the list they started with. For a special service, make another list and choose it for that service. Make one as **An empty list**, or **A copy of** another.\n\nHiding a list keeps it under **Hidden lists**. You can't hide the default.\n\n## Making a checklist\n\n1. **Add a checklist** to a department. One per kind of position works well, such as Camera Operators.\n2. **Paste a list:** one item on each line. Start a line with `#` to begin a section. Bullets and numbers are removed for you. You can also add sections and items one at a time.\n3. **Who it's for:** choose the positions. People scheduled in these positions see this checklist first. Everyone serving can still see the rest.\n\n## Changing one\n\nUse **Edit, move or remove** on any section or item. If you remove a task, it disappears from current and future checklists. Past services keep their records, and you can bring it back from **Hidden items**.\n\n## Hidden items\n\nAnything you remove shows up here, newest first, with **Restore**. If what something belongs to is hidden too, restore that first.\n\n## Activity log\n\n**Activity log** shows every tick, untick, reset, and undo at a service, including attempts that were refused, and every change to the checklists. It can't be edited or deleted. Use it to work out what happened.\n\n## Service history\n\n**Service history** shows what was done and what was missed at each service. A service's record is fixed when its day ends, so later checklist changes never alter it.\n\n## Checklist settings\n\nFor church-wide checklist managers:\n\n- **Service type:** the checklist opens to the next service of this type. Other services still have checklists.\n- **Service day:** when nothing is published yet, the checklist still opens for this day so the team can tick. When Planning Center publishes the service, everything ticked stays with it.\n\n## If someone can't see their checklist\n\nUsually their Planning Center position isn't linked. The Task lists page warns you about it. Link it on the [Planning Center page](admin-scheduling.md).\n",
  "admin-curriculum": "# Admin: curriculum\n\n**Curriculum** is where you build courses, for a general category or for a level in a department. You publish your own department's work. Nobody else has to approve it, unless your department has turned on review before publishing.\n\n## General categories and departments\n\nThe row of buttons at the top starts with the **general categories**, then a divider, then the **departments**.\n\n- A **department** is a team. Its courses sit in three levels, and they can have practical assessments.\n- A **general category**, such as General or Leadership, is courses for anyone on the production team. Some use levels, and some are one list you can put in order with the arrows. They have lessons, quizzes, versions, and certificates, but no practical assessments, so the Practicals tab doesn't appear.\n\nProduction Directors build and publish these. You add one under [Departments](admin-people.md#departments).\n\n## Topics on courses and lessons\n\n**Topics** are tags, such as Mixing or Directing, that help people find things: **Topic** is a filter on the **Courses** and **Explore** pages. Open a lesson and click its topics in the box at the top, or open a course's **Details** tab to do the same for the course. **+ New topic** adds one.\n\nA topic on a course shows that course. It doesn't list all its lessons as single videos: tag a lesson for that. A lesson's topics also bring up its course. They save straight away with no draft, because they aren't part of a version, and they stay when you publish a new one.\n\nDepartment Leaders, Production Directors, and Platform Administrators can add topics, there or under **Topics**. Production Directors and Platform Administrators can also choose a topic on that page to rename or remove it. Removing a topic takes it off every course and lesson.\n\n## Start here\n\nTurn on **Start here** beside a course in General. If you can publish it, the course then shows at the top of Training until someone finishes it, on each department's page, and beside each name on Serving teams as Done or Not yet. It never stops anyone joining or serving.\n\n## Course covers\n\nOpen a course's **Details** tab to upload its cover: upright, about 900 by 1200 pixels, as a PNG, JPEG, or WebP up to 2 MB. A cover isn't part of a version, so it shows straight away, with no draft to publish. Until a course has one, it shows its title on its category's colour.\n\n## Formatting text\n\nThe course summary, lesson summary, and lesson text work like a word processor. Use the buttons above the text for **Bold**, **Italic**, **Underline**, **Heading**, **Subheading**, lists, and **Link**. Links need to start with `https://`.\n\nIf you paste text that has `#` or `**` markers, they turn into real formatting. Volunteers see the same formatting on the course and lesson pages. Summaries can be up to 5,000 characters and lesson text up to 20,000, and the count under each box shows how much you've used.\n\n## The key idea: drafts and versions\n\nChanges stay in a draft until you publish, and volunteers keep seeing the live version until then. Each publish is kept as a version you can go back to. People's progress stays linked to the version they earned it on, so publishing never rewrites anyone's history.\n\n## Making a course\n\n1. Choose **Add course** under a level, and give it a title.\n2. Fill in **Course details:** a summary and objectives.\n3. Add a module, then add lessons to it.\n4. Add a quiz and a practical if the course needs them.\n5. **Publish,** with a change note saying what changed.\n\nTo change a published course, choose **Edit course**. That starts a new draft.\n\nIf you make a course by mistake and it's never been published, you can delete it from its row's menu. A course people have learned from can only be archived: volunteers stop seeing it, and its versions and everyone's history are kept.\n\n## Lessons\n\n| Field | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Title, module, summary** | What it is and where it sits |\n| **Objectives** | One per line. Shown as \"By the end of this lesson\" |\n| **Estimated minutes** | Optional |\n| **Video** | Optional. Either YouTube (unlisted) or the church video library. For YouTube, paste the link from the Share button |\n| **Resources** | Attach this department's SOPs. The lesson always shows each SOP's current version |\n| **Required to complete the course** | Leave it on unless the lesson is optional |\n\nLearners mark lessons complete themselves. The app never claims a video was fully watched.\n\n## Quizzes\n\nSet where the quiz sits, and its rules: the pass mark (80% to start with), how many attempts (3 to start with), whether correct answers are shown, whether questions shuffle, and whether it must be passed to finish the course.\n\nAdd questions with **Add question**. A quiz without questions can't be published. A question has a type, its answers, which one is correct, and an optional explanation shown with the correct answers when the quiz allows.\n\nThe app scores every attempt. Nobody marks them by hand. If someone runs out of attempts, open **Results** and choose **Give another attempt**, with a note such as \"Went through camera presets together\". They get a notification that they can try again.\n\n## Practical assessments\n\nA practical is a checklist an observer ticks while watching someone work.\n\n1. **Add to checklist:** something they can watch happen, not a feeling, such as \"Frames the speaker with headroom\". Mark each item as required or not.\n2. **Settings:** where it sits, which competency a pass grants, and the pass rule. The usual rule is that every required item must be observed.\n\nThe observer ticks what they saw, and the Academy works out **Pass** or **Not yet** from your rule. See [Assessments and competencies](admin-assessments.md).\n",
  "admin-onboarding": "# Admin: onboarding\n\nOnboarding takes someone from \"I want to serve\" to ready for a team. You build each pathway's stages and steps. People move along by themselves, and you're told when they need you.\n\n## How people get in\n\n- **The sign-up page:** the public **Serve with us** page. Under **People in pathways**, choose **Share the sign-up page** to print it for the lobby or show it at a volunteer night, with a QR code.\n- **You add them:** **Add someone**, for someone you met in the lobby. They start at the pathway's first step.\n- **Joining a department** can start that department's pathway.\n\n## Building a pathway\n\nSet up **Join the team** first: everyone who wants to serve starts there, then moves on to a department's pathway.\n\nOpen **Onboarding pathways**. Add stages, such as \"Week 1\". Then choose **Add a step** to open the **Step library**, and drag steps onto a stage, or press **Add**. Select a step to change what it does. A step marked **Needs setup** isn't finished, and **Waits here** means people pause on it until it's done.\n\nThe steps you can use:\n\n| Step | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Send an email** | Start from a template, then edit the subject and message. `{first name}` becomes their first name. The email goes out within about 15 minutes of reaching this step. Emails written before onboarding emails were switched on (9 November 2026) were never sent. |\n| **Notify leaders** | Tells the leaders who look after the pathway |\n| **Follow-up task** | Something a leader has to do, with who reaches out and how many hours they have |\n| **Pair a mentor** | Sets up [mentoring](mentoring.md) |\n| **Assign a course** | Moves on by itself when they finish it |\n| **Wait some days** | Pauses before the next step |\n| **Choose a department** | The volunteer picks one on Training, under **Your next steps**. Leave departments unticked to offer every open one, including ones opened later |\n| **Add to a team in Planning Center** | A task for a leader, which completes itself when the sync sees them on the team. Planning Center stays read-only |\n| **Finish** | Ends the pathway |\n\nTo remove a stage, move or remove its steps first.\n\n## Version history\n\nChanges save as you make them, and each sitting is kept as a version: all your changes within half an hour count as one. Open **Version history** beside **Rename or describe** to see each version, when it was saved, who by, and how many stages and steps it had.\n\nTo go back, choose **Restore this version** under the one you want. It first lists what restoring would do: steps it brings back, steps it takes out, and steps whose settings it puts back. Choose **Restore version** to do it. The pathway goes back as it was, and that's saved as a new version, so you can undo a restore the same way. Nothing is deleted, and people already in the pathway carry on from where they are.\n\n## Follow-ups\n\n**Follow-ups** is what onboarding needs from you: **Yours**, **Anyone can take**, and **With other leaders**. Choose **I'll take this**, **Hand over** to someone else, or **Mark done**. Ticking one moves that person on to their next step.\n\n## People in pathways\n\n**People in pathways** shows who's where, how long they've been there, and what happens next. Open someone for their history and notes. A note can be shared with leaders who look after the pathway, or private to you.\n\nIf someone changes their mind, choose **Stop onboarding**. Their history and notes are kept, and you can add them again later.\n\n## What it doesn't do\n\nA pathway never adds anyone to a team and never approves anyone to serve. Courses only move a step on from real progress.\n",
  "admin-overview": "# Admin: start here\n\nIf you lead something, **Admin** is where you do it. Open your name, then **Admin**. The sidebar only shows what your role allows, and every page checks that again on the server. If a page says it isn't open to you, your role doesn't include it.\n\n**Back to Academy** is the purple button at the top of the sidebar. One section of the sidebar stays open at a time, and **Collapse all** closes it. A small dot on a closed section means you're in it, or something in it is waiting for you. The switch beside your name changes between light and dark.\n\n## The Dashboard\n\nThe first page answers two questions.\n\n- **Needs you:** things only you can move forward, such as requests waiting or assessments to do. Each one opens the page where you do it.\n- **Training, by department:** read each row left to right. People join, learn, get checked, then serve. A big drop between two numbers shows where people are getting stuck.\n\nBelow that is recent activity from the audit log.\n\nIf Planning Center hasn't synced for an hour, an orange warning sits at the top for people who run the teams: \"Schedules haven't updated from Planning Center since…\". **Try again now** asks for a sync straight away. It goes away by itself once a sync works, and admins are emailed once when it stops and once when it's working again.\n\n## Who can do what\n\n| Role | What it's for |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Volunteer** | Learns and serves. No admin abilities |\n| **Trainer or Evaluator** | Watches practical assessments for their department |\n| **Department Leader** | Runs one department: members, curriculum, SOPs, assessments, reports |\n| **Production Director** | The same across every department, plus choosing Service Dashboard editors |\n| **Scheduling Administrator** | Schedules and teams |\n| **Platform Administrator** | Accounts, roles, departments, and app settings, and drafting and publishing training and SOPs. It doesn't approve memberships |\n| **Service Dashboard Editor** | Changes the dashboard layout. Up to 3 people |\n\nSomeone can hold different roles in different departments, such as leading Audio while learning in Camera. You give out roles under [People and roles](admin-people.md).\n\n## How the app decides things\n\n- **The system decides results, not people.** Quizzes score themselves. A practical passes when the observer's checklist meets your pass rule, and the observer never picks the outcome. A pass grants the competency, and Approved to Serve follows automatically.\n- **Teams decide who serves, not training.** Training shows beside each name, and never blocks anyone.\n- **History is never rewritten.** People's progress stays linked to the version they earned it on.\n- **Sensitive changes are logged.** See the [audit log](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md).\n\n## Where things are\n\n| Sidebar group | Pages | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Checklists** | Task lists, Activity log, Service history, Hidden items, Checklist settings | [Checklists](admin-checklists.md) |\n| **Services & Scheduling** | Planning Center, Serving teams, Schedule | [Scheduling](admin-scheduling.md) |\n| **Services & Scheduling** | Service Dashboard setup | [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Curriculum | [Curriculum](admin-curriculum.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | SOP library | [SOP library](admin-sops.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Assessments, Competencies and roles | [Assessments and competencies](admin-assessments.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Membership requests, Departments | [People and departments](admin-people.md) |\n| **Training Academy** | Mentoring, Reports | [Reports, mentoring, and the audit log](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md) |\n| **People & Teams** | People and roles, Invitations | [People and departments](admin-people.md) |\n| **People & Teams** | Onboarding pathways, People in pathways, Follow-ups | [Onboarding](admin-onboarding.md) |\n| **System & Settings** | Audit log | [Reports, mentoring, and the audit log](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md) |\n| **System & Settings** | App name and logo | [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) |\n| **System & Settings** | Features | This guide, under Features below |\n\n## Problem reports\n\nAnyone can tell the team something isn't working: open your name, then **Report a problem**. **Admin → Problem reports** lists what people sent, newest first, for Platform Administrators. Each report shows who sent it and when, and the page, device, and app version it was sent from, so you can find the problem. Platform Administrators also get a notification for each one.\n\nChoose **Mark as sorted** once it's dealt with, or **Reopen** if it comes back. **Open** shows what's still to do and **All** shows everything. The number beside **Problem reports** in the sidebar is how many are open. Each change goes in the audit log. Someone can send up to 10 reports a day.\n\n## Features\n\n**Admin → Features** switches parts of the app on or off for everyone. Only Platform Administrators see it.\n\nWhile a feature is off, nobody sees it, and the app refuses to let anyone use it, even someone who knows the address. Switching one on or off takes effect straight away and goes in the audit log, with who did it.\n\n| Feature | What it adds |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Messages** | Department chats, group chats, and direct messages with photos, GIFs and documents. Under it, choose how long photos and documents (30 to 365 days) and messages (1 to 10 years) are kept. A daily cleanup removes older ones, so the Academy stays inside the free plan |\n",
  "admin-people": "# Admin: people and departments\n\n## Membership requests\n\n**Membership requests** lists people asking to join your department, oldest first. Choose **Approve**. To add a note or decline, choose **More**. A note or a reason for declining is optional, and it's kept in the history. Either way, they get a notification. Your reason stays with leaders and is never sent to them.\n\nApproving makes someone part of the department's team. It doesn't approve them to serve. Courses are already open to anyone.\n\nThe **Members** view lists who's in each department. To take someone out, choose **Remove**. Their progress and history are kept. They get a notification.\n\n## Invitations\n\nUse **Invitations** to bring someone straight into a department. They join when they accept, because you've already decided.\n\n1. Choose **New invitation**.\n2. Pick a department, or **The Academy only, no department**.\n3. Say how many people can use it and how long the link works. A note for you is optional. Leave out personal details.\n4. Copy the link, or show its QR code on a screen or print it. **It's shown only once,** for security.\n\nThe person opens the link and signs in with **Planning Center**, or with an email link if they aren't on Planning Center. Either way they come back to the invitation and choose **Accept**. Nothing is emailed for you, so send the link yourself, for example by text.\n\nTo stop a link, choose **Revoke**. It stops working straight away, and people who already joined stay members.\n\n## Phone alerts and app\n\n**Phone alerts and app** shows who on your teams has the Academy on their phone and alerts turned on, with anyone to follow up first. Department Leaders see their own departments. Production Directors and Platform Admins see everyone. Your own **Notification settings** also has a **Your teams** card that leads here.\n\nAt the top are four numbers: how many have the app, how many have alerts on, how many have some alerts off, and how many don't have alerts on. Choose **Everyone**, **No app**, **Alerts not on**, **Some alerts off** or **Phone can't** to narrow the list. On a phone, each person has two small marks, **App** and **Alerts**. On a computer, each has a column.\n\n| Shown as | What it means |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Installed** | They've opened the Academy from their Home Screen, on the device shown |\n| **Not installed** | They use the website, or haven't opened the installed app yet |\n| **On** | Alerts are on for a phone or tablet |\n| **Some off** | Alerts are on, but they've switched some off for their phone, shown as \"Off for: Training\" |\n| **Not on** | No alerts on any device yet |\n| **Computer only** | Alerts are on a computer but not a phone |\n| **Phone can't** | They've said their phone can't do alerts. They get emails instead |\n\nOnly each person can install the app or turn alerts on, so someone listed as **Not installed** or **Not on** may need a hand. On an iPhone, the Academy has to be on the Home Screen first. The app is counted once someone opens it from their Home Screen, and a phone doesn't say when an app is deleted. Someone can also switch alerts off in their phone's settings without the Academy knowing until an alert fails to reach them, so **On** means the last we heard.\n\n## People and roles\n\n**People and roles** lists everyone who has signed in, 50 to a page. Open a person to:\n\n- **Grant a role,** choosing a department if the role needs one. Pick a role to see what it lets someone do. They get a notification saying which role, where, and who gave it.\n- **Remove a role,** with a reason. Past roles stay listed. They're told the role was removed.\n- **Change their account:** Suspend, Deactivate, or Reactivate. Training history is kept whatever the status. On the People list, only accounts that aren't active have a label beside the name.\n\nYou can't change your own account status or remove your own roles, so one person can't lock everyone out by accident. Every change goes in the audit log.\n\nThe Service Dashboard Editor role is limited to 3 people, here as well as on the [dashboard's setup page](service-dashboard.md).\n\n## Departments\n\n**Departments** is for adding, opening, pausing, or renaming a department. Changes take effect straight away and go in the audit log. Nobody's training history changes.\n\n**General categories** have their own list lower on the page. A general category, such as General or Leadership, is courses only: nobody joins it, and it has no positions or practical assessments, so it doesn't appear in membership requests, invitations, teams, or reports. Choose **Add a general category** to add one, and tick **Use three levels** if its courses should be in levels.\n\nA new department gets the three levels straight away. For each one, you choose:\n\n| Setting | Choices |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Status** | Open now. Coming soon, which is seen but can't be joined. Hidden while you set it up |\n| **How people join** | They ask and a leader approves, anyone can join with no approval, or by invitation only. Joining never approves someone to serve. If you remove someone from a department anyone can join, they have to ask to come back |\n\nLighting Operations goes live just by changing its status here. No new release is needed.\n",
  "admin-reports-mentoring-audit": "# Admin: reports, mentoring, and the audit log\n\n## Reports\n\n**Reports** covers departments where you can view training reports. Choose a department and what to show:\n\n- **Course completion by volunteer:** who's where in each course, with certificates.\n- **Competencies and Approved to Serve by volunteer:** what each person holds.\n- **Approved to Serve by department and role:** how many people are ready for each role. Aim for at least two approved people in every role, so one absence doesn't leave a gap.\n\n- **Course progress:** pick a course and see how many people have finished, are in progress, or haven't started. Choose a number to filter the list. Choose a person to see the lessons they've marked complete, their best quiz score and how many tries it took, and their practical. This tab also covers General and Leadership.\n\nA finished course is separate from being approved to serve, and the app never says a video was watched, only which lessons someone marked complete.\n\nReports include the department's members, anyone else who has started training there, and anyone assigned a course there, because anyone can take any course. None of that makes someone a member or puts them on a team.\n\n### Assigning a course\n\nOn **Course progress**, choose **Assign to someone**, search for the person, choose them, then **Assign**. The course shows under **Assigned to you** on their Training page until they finish it.\n\nYou can also assign from the course itself: open it, choose **Actions** at the top right, then **Assign to someone**. Each name shows whether they're already assigned or how many lessons they've done. Pick a due date if you want one (**This Sunday**, **In 2 weeks**, or **Pick a date**), then **Assign**. Choosing someone who's already assigned changes their due date instead, and they're told. Only leaders see **Assign to someone**.\n\nIt's a request and nothing more. It doesn't stop them doing anything else, add them to a team, or count towards being approved to serve. Anyone can still take any course without being assigned it.\n\nTo undo one, choose the person, then **Remove assignment**. Anything they've already done stays. Assigning and removing both go in the audit log. They're told either way.\n\n**Download CSV** gives you the same data as a file. Downloads go in the audit log. They include names and training records only, and never mentoring's private notes.\n\n## Mentoring\n\n**Mentoring** pairs a volunteer with a trainer or leader. Choose the mentor and the volunteer, then **Pair them**. Pairing needs at least one trainer or leader and one member in the department.\n\nThey share goals and notes. You can read the shared ones. **A mentor's private notes stay private, even from you.**\n\nTo finish, choose **End this pairing**, with an optional reason. It stays on record for both of them. Both of them are told it has ended. See [Mentoring](mentoring.md) for what they see.\n\n## Audit log\n\n**Audit log** lists every sensitive change, newest first: who did it, what happened, and when, in Winnipeg time. Entries can't be edited or deleted.\n\nUse it to answer \"who changed this, and when?\" It includes things like roles, memberships, publishing, overrides, report downloads, and Service Dashboard layouts and screens.\n",
  "admin-scheduling": "# Admin: scheduling\n\nPlanning Center Services schedules, and the Academy reads from it. The Academy never changes anything in Planning Center.\n\n## Planning Center\n\nThe **Planning Center** page is where the two connect.\n\n- **The top of the page:** the key numbers from the last read, which happens every 15 minutes. Choose **Refresh now** if you've just made a change and don't want to wait. If no read has worked for an hour, an orange warning says since when, with **Try again now**. It goes away by itself once a read works.\n- **Position links:** connect each Planning Center position to an Academy position, so people see the right checklist and their training. Your choice saves as soon as you pick it. On a phone, tap a row to choose. Planning Center gives each service type its own copy of a team, so link each one. Add Academy positions under [Competencies and roles](admin-assessments.md) first.\n- **Scheduled in positions not linked yet:** these people won't see their checklist for the service until you link their position.\n- **Not in the Academy yet:** people on a Planning Center team or schedule who haven't signed in. Nothing is sent to them. They get an account the first time they sign in with Planning Center. While the Academy schedules, anyone in the church's Planning Center can sign in this way, and you add them to teams here.\n- **Who schedules:** Planning Center, or the Academy's own tools. Leave it on Planning Center unless you've decided to move away from it. While the Academy schedules, Planning Center Services isn't read at all, so nothing there changes the schedule here.\n\n### Switching who schedules\n\nOpen **Change this** under Who schedules. Before you switch to the Academy, it shows what will be copied from Planning Center: service types, upcoming services, who's scheduled with their replies, team members, and blockout dates. Choose **Switch to the Academy** and they become the Academy's own, so nothing needs to be typed in again. Past services aren't touched, and nobody is emailed.\n\nSwitching back to Planning Center hands those upcoming services back, and the next read updates them from Planning Center. Services you made in the Academy stay. Anything you changed in the Academy meanwhile isn't sent to Planning Center, so make it there too. Each switch is in the audit log.\n\nThe setup is in the [Planning Center setup guide](../runbooks/scheduling.md).\n\n## Serving teams\n\n**Serving teams** shows who serves in each position, with their training, and a column for each Start here course (Done or Not yet), and what each position sees on the day. These are there to help you, and they never stop anyone being added. While Planning Center schedules, you add people to positions there, and they appear here after the next read.\n\nEach position has settings for the day:\n\n- **Sees all departments:** opens every department's checklist first.\n- **Leads the service:** can reset the checklists.\n- **Gets the Director role while scheduled.**\n\n## Schedule\n\n**Schedule** has two views while Planning Center schedules:\n\n- **Coming up:** each service, and how many positions are filled.\n- **Calendar:** every service this month. Tap a day to see who's serving and what's still open.\n\nTo change a service or who's on it, do it in Planning Center. It shows here within 15 minutes.\n\n## If the Academy schedules instead\n\nIf **Who schedules** is set to the Academy, you also get **Matrix** and **Service types**:\n\n1. **Service types:** set up each kind of service once, with its time, positions, and **Reminder email** (how many days before, 2 unless you change it).\n2. **Make services:** create the next weeks in one step, repeating every 1 to 4 weeks. Dates that already have this service are skipped.\n3. **Matrix:** the next services side by side. Open a cell to schedule someone. **Auto-schedule open spots** suggests someone for each open place, with the reason beside each name: only the position's team, never anyone blocked out, serving that day, or who declined that place, within how often each person asked to serve, with or apart from their household as they asked, trained first, then whoever served least recently. Nobody is added until you choose **Keep these**, and nobody is emailed until you send requests. **Discard** clears the suggestions.\n4. **Edit details:** on a service's page, change its name, day, times, location, or notes. If the day or start time changes, everyone on it gets a short email.\n\n**New service** on Schedule makes one service: choose the day, the **Service type** (such as Sunday Service, Holy Communion Service, or Thanksgiving Service), and the **Plan**. The type brings its positions, time, and reminder. The plan brings the order of service, and it's the type's usual plan unless you choose another.\n\n### Order of service and plan templates\n\nOn a service's page, **Order of service and times** opens its plan:\n\n- **Items:** items, songs, and section headers, each with a length such as 4:30. The running time and the clock time each item starts add up as you type. Use the arrows to reorder. **Add note** puts a note on an item for one department, such as \"Cam 1 tight on the speaker\" for Camera Operations. Choose **Save order of service** when you're done. The Service Dashboard and the wall TV show it straight away.\n- **Use this plan** replaces the order with a plan template's, and you can change anything after.\n- **Save as a template** keeps this order for other services.\n- **Other times:** rehearsals and sound checks on the same day. They show in requests, reminders, and calendars.\n- **Files:** stage plots, run sheets, and the like, up to 10 MB each (PDF, images, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, or text). Only schedulers and the people asked to serve at that service can open them.\n\n**Plan templates**, a tab on Schedule, lists the saved orders of service. Open one to change it, **Duplicate** it, or **Archive** it. Changing a template never changes services already planned. On a service type, **Usual plan** chooses the template its new services start with.\n\nWhile Planning Center schedules, a service's order of service is shown read only, as Planning Center has it.\n\nOn a service's page:\n\n- **Places:** each position shows how many people it needs, such as \"Camera 2, 1 of 2 filled\". **Need one more** adds a place and **Need one fewer** removes an empty one. **Add position** adds one the service doesn't have yet.\n- **Add someone:** only the position's team is listed. Someone blocked out, or already in another position that day, can still be added. A **Conflict** note then shows their other position or their blockout note, as in Planning Center. Nothing is blocked. You can pair someone still learning with **Someone alongside**.\n- **Blockouts:** volunteers block out one day, a date range, part of a day, or a repeating day such as every 1st Sunday. A part-of-a-day blockout only counts if it overlaps the service. If someone blocks out a service they're already on, you're told.\n- **Not sent yet:** people you add aren't emailed straight away. When you're ready, **Send requests** emails each person once, for everything they're on at that service, with your message if you add one. Untick anyone you want to hold back. Each send is in the audit log.\n- **Reminders to confirm** go 48 hours after the request was sent, if there's still no reply.\n- **Reminder email:** choose how many days before the service (1 to 7) everyone who accepted gets their \"Serving soon\" reminder, at 10:00 a.m. The page shows the day it goes out. Set the usual number on the service type, and change it for one service on that service's page.\n- **When someone declines,** you're told, and the Academy adds the best free person from that position's team and asks them. That's someone who isn't blocked out or serving that day and hasn't already declined, trained first, then whoever served least recently. Their place shows \"Added automatically after … declined\". If nobody is free, the place stays open and you're told that instead.\n",
  "admin-sops": "# Admin: SOP library\n\n**SOP library** holds the procedures for the departments you look after, and the ones for all of production.\n\n## Finding an SOP\n\nSOPs are grouped by category. Select a category's heading to collapse or open it, or use the category buttons to show one. You can also search, and filter by who it's for and its status. If any SOPs are due for review, a note at the top can show just those.\n\n## Adding an SOP\n\n1. Choose **Add SOP**. Give it a title, say who it's for, and choose a category or create a new one.\n2. Write the steps in the page, attach a file, or do both. A file can be a PDF, PNG, or JPEG up to 10 MB, and it's stored privately.\n3. Add a summary saying what it's for and when to use it. **Save draft** keeps your work.\n4. Choose **Publish** and say what changed. The draft becomes the current version.\n\nAn SOP needs written steps or a file before it can be published.\n\n## Writing the steps\n\nType in the page, and use the buttons above it to add headings, numbered steps, bullets, bold, and warnings. **Use a template** gives you a starting shape, and **Preview** shows what volunteers will see.\n\n## SOPs for all of production\n\nChoose **All of production** for a procedure that isn't about one department, such as how to do a copyright review. Everyone signed in can read the current version.\n\nOnly someone with organisation-wide rights, such as the Production Director or a Platform Administrator, can add or change these. Department leaders can attach them to their lessons.\n\n## Changing an SOP\n\nChoose **Edit SOP**. That starts a draft from the current version, and members keep seeing the current one until you publish. Say what changed. The previous version is kept and marked as replaced, and only the current version can be opened, so nobody follows an old procedure by mistake.\n\n## Keeping them current\n\nUnder **Settings**, set the owner, category, and how often it needs reviewing. When a review comes up, the owner is reminded.\n\nIf you've checked the current version and it's still right, choose to confirm the review. That doesn't need a new version.\n\n## Archiving\n\nChoose **Archive** at the top of an SOP's page when it's no longer in use. Members stop seeing it, and lessons stop listing it. Its versions and files are kept.\n\nArchived SOPs are hidden from the library until you set the status filter to **Archived**. Open one there, and **Delete** at the top removes it for good, with all its versions and files. It can't be undone, though the audit log keeps a note of what was deleted. If a live or draft course still lists the SOP, remove it from that lesson first.\n\n## Attaching to lessons\n\nAttach SOPs to a lesson under [Curriculum](admin-curriculum.md). The lesson always shows the current version, so you only update it in one place.\n",
  "checklist": "# Checklist\n\n**Checklist** is what each position does before and after a service. It opens to today's service, or the next one.\n\nWhen Messages is switched on, phones show the checklist under **Serve** on the bottom bar. The **SOPs** button at the top of the checklist opens the SOPs.\n\n## Using it\n\nAt the top, a calendar tile shows the service's date, with the day of the week, the time, and the place beside it.\n\nWhen you're scheduled, the **Checklist** button on your serving card on Training, and **Open checklist** on Schedule, take you to that service with your position's checklist open. If you serve in two positions, each one opens its own.\n\nYour own department's checklist comes first, marked **YOU**. On a computer, choose another department from the list on the left to see theirs. On a phone or iPad, the button at the top names the checklist you're looking at: tap it, and a sheet slides up listing every checklist with how much is done. Tap one to open it, or drag the sheet down to close it. Above the list, **All tasks** and **Still to do** each show how many items they hold; the one that's on is purple. **Still to do** hides what's already ticked. Tick items as you go. The tick shows straight away and saves in the background. If a save fails, the tick goes back and tells you why.\n\nEveryone sees each other's ticks within about 30 seconds, without reloading.\n\n## When you can tick\n\nTicking opens two days before the service and closes twelve hours after it. Outside that, you can read it but not tick.\n\n## Who can see it\n\nThe Checklist is for the media team. Your access comes from your team in Planning Center. If you can't open it and think you should, ask a scheduling administrator to add you to a team.\n\n## If you're leading the service\n\nPositions that lead the service, such as Technical Director, can also:\n\n- **Reset checklists:** clears every tick for this service, for when it was ticked by mistake. The ticks are kept in the record.\n- **Undo the last reset:** brings those ticks back. Anything ticked again since stays as it is.\n\n## On the Service Dashboard\n\nThe [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) has a Checklist tile. A tick there is the same tick as here, with the same rules. The wall TV only shows totals.\n\n## After the service\n\nEach service keeps its own record of what was done and what was missed. Every service starts fresh. Leaders can see past records under [Admin](admin-checklists.md).\n",
  "getting-started": "# Getting started\n\nThe Academy is where the production team at Immanuel Fellowship Church learns, gets ready, and sees what's happening on a Sunday. This page gets you signed in and shows you around.\n\n## Signing in\n\n- **If you're on a production team in Planning Center,** choose the Planning Center button. You don't need a separate password.\n- **If you're not,** choose **Sign in with an email link**, type your email, and open the link we send you. It works once. There's no password to remember.\n- **If you'd like to join the team,** choose **Join the production team** on the sign-in page and fill in the form. You don't need an account for it.\n\nYou can read these guides without signing in: choose **Help and guides** on the sign-in page. Choose **Sign in** at the top to go back.\n\n## Your profile\n\nOpen your name, then choose your name at the top of the menu, to see the quizzes you've passed, how many lessons you've completed, and your best score on each quiz. Open a quiz to see each try. Only you and your leaders can see these. They don't decide whether you serve.\n\n## Finding your way around\n\nOn a tablet or computer, the side bar on the left lists these places, each an icon with its name, under **Learn** and **Serve**. The small round arrow on the side bar's right edge, which shows when you point at the side bar, folds it down to icons only (hover over an icon to see its name), and the device remembers your choice. On a phone, five of them are on the bar floating along the bottom: a soft purple highlight slides to the one you're on. **Explore** is at the top of **Courses** there.\n\n| Place | What it's for | Guide |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Training** | Departments, courses, and how far you've got | [Training](training.md) |\n| **Courses, Explore** | The Library: find any course or video, with search and filters | [Training](training.md) |\n| **Schedule** | When you're serving, and who with | [Schedule](schedule.md) |\n| **Checklist** | What to do before and after this service | [Checklist](checklist.md) |\n| **SOPs** | The team's written procedures | [SOPs](sops.md) |\n| **Messages** | Chats with your department, groups, and anyone in the Academy, once it's switched on | [Messages](messages.md) |\n\nWhen Messages is switched on, it sits under **Training** in the side bar, with how many are unread, and in the middle of the bar along the bottom of a phone. On a phone, **Checklist** and **SOPs** then share one tab, **Serve**, which opens the checklist and has an **SOPs** button at the top.\n\nIf you're on a production team and using a computer, you also get the [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md).\n\nBeside your name is the bell (notifications). On a phone your name, the bell, and the sun or moon (light or dark) are at the top right. On a tablet or computer your name and the bell are at the bottom of the side bar, and light or dark is in the menu your name opens. Your name opens a menu with the rest: choose your name at the top of it for your profile, then mentoring, getting the app, help, and signing out. Leaders also see **Admin** there.\n\n## Three things that are kept separate\n\nPeople often assume these are one thing. They aren't.\n\n1. **Finishing a course** means you did the lessons and passed the quiz.\n2. **A competency** means someone watched you do the job and you met the checklist.\n3. **Approved to Serve** follows by itself once you hold every competency a role needs.\n\nWatching videos alone never approves you to serve. And being approved doesn't put you on the schedule: team managers decide who serves, and they can add you while you're still learning.\n\n## Your profile\n\nOpen your name, then choose your name at the top of the menu. Your profile shows the name leaders see, with **Edit name** under it, then three totals (lessons done, quizzes passed, and roles you're approved in), **Approved to serve** for each role, your departments and how far you've got, your quizzes, your certificates, and how you sign in. If you signed in with Planning Center and your name differs there, you can use that one.\n\n## Notifications\n\nNotifications come to your phone and email as you've chosen, and they're all in the app too. Choose the bell beside your name. On a phone a dot on it means something is unread; in the side bar it shows how many. The bell opens a panel with your newest notifications, such as being scheduled or an assessment result. On a phone it pulls down from under the top bar, with a point at the bell. Choose the bell again, anywhere outside the panel, or the handle at its foot to close it.\n\nNew notifications are listed first, under **New**, and the rest under **Earlier**. When there's more than one kind, choose **All**, **Serving**, **Training** or **Leading** at the top to see just those.\n\nEach notification has an icon for what it's about (serving, training, or leading) and a line in purple saying what tapping it does, such as **Confirm or decline**.\n\nYou're also told when a leader changes something for you: a role given or removed, a request to join turned down, being removed from a department, a skill paused, a certificate withdrawn, another try at a quiz, being taken off a service, mentoring ending, or a course no longer assigned.\n\n- **Mark all read** clears the dot and keeps your notifications. It's greyed out when everything is already read.\n- **Clear all** takes every notification out of the list, after asking you first. To clear just one, choose the **×** beside it. Cleared notifications don't come back, and new ones still arrive as usual.\n- **See all notifications** opens the full list.\n- The settings button (the sliders) opens **Notification settings**.\n\n### Notification settings\n\nHere you turn on alerts for the device you're using, and choose what also reaches your phone or your email. There's one choice for each group: **Serving**, **Training**, for leaders **Leading**, and, when Messages is switched on, **Messages**. Each has a **Phone** button and an **Email** button, except Messages, which is never emailed and has only **Phone**. A tick means it's on. A dash means only some kinds in that group are on.\n\nTo choose kinds one by one, open **Fine-tune each alert**. Most people never need it.\n\nNew courses, certificates, and SOP updates stay in the app. Once your phone has alerts on, you only get an email if a service is moved or cancelled, or for anything you've turned off for your phone. Every email has a link back to these settings.\n\n## Put it on your phone\n\nOpen your name, then **Get the app**. It's free, takes a minute, and doesn't use an app store.\n\n- **iPhone or iPad:** open the Academy in Safari, tap **Share**, then **Add to Home Screen**, then **Add**.\n- **Android:** open the Academy in Chrome and tap **Install app**.\n- **Computer:** in Chrome or Edge, select the install icon at the right of the address bar.\n\nThe app keeps itself up to date. When a new version is out, it loads it the next time you come back to the app, or when you move to another page if you're in the middle of typing. You never need to delete it and add it again.\n\n### Alerts\n\nOn the same page, choose **Turn on alerts** to get updates that drop down like any other app. If you're on a team, the app asks you to do this on your phone each time you open it, until it's done. **Not now** puts it off until next time, and nothing is locked in the meantime. If your phone can't do alerts, choose **My phone can't do this**. Your leaders will see that, and you'll get emails instead. You turn alerts on separately for each device, and they come at any time of day (your phone's Do Not Disturb still applies). On iPhone and iPad, alerts need iOS 16.4 or later, and they only work once the Academy is on your Home Screen.\n\n### The number on the app's icon\n\nOnce the Academy is on your Home Screen, its icon shows how many notifications and messages you haven't read yet, added together, like other apps. It goes down as you read them, and it updates when an alert arrives, even with the app closed. On iPhone and iPad it needs iOS 16.4 or later and alerts turned on. On an installed computer app it shows on the taskbar or dock. Android shows its own dot on the icon instead of a number.\n\n## Dark or light\n\nOn a phone, tap the sun or moon beside your name to change between light and dark: one tap switches. On a tablet or computer, open your name and use the **Dark mode** switch at the top of the menu. It's remembered on each device.\n\n## Want to join the team?\n\nIf you're not serving yet, the **Serve with us** page is the place to start. Tell us your name and what interests you, and a leader will be in touch within a few days. No experience is needed.\n\n## Getting more help\n\nOpen your name, then **Help**, from any page. If you're on a screen with its own guide, that's where it opens. For anything else, ask your department leader or the Production Director.\n\nIf something isn't working, open your name, then **Report a problem**. Say what happened. Your note goes to the team with the page you were on, your kind of phone or computer, and the app's version, and nothing else, so they can find it. You'll see a thank-you once it's sent.\n",
  "mentoring": "# Mentoring\n\nA department leader can pair you with a mentor: a trainer or leader who helps you grow. To find it, open your name, then **Mentoring**. If you'd like a mentor and don't have one, ask your department leader.\n\n## What's in a pairing\n\n| Part | Who can read it |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Goals** | Both of you and the department's leaders |\n| **Shared notes** | Both of you and the department's leaders |\n| **Your private notes** | Only you. They never appear in reports or exports |\n\nAdd a goal with an optional follow-up date. Add shared notes for anything you'd like to keep together, such as feedback after a service.\n\nWhen a pairing ends, it stays here, marked as ended, for reference. You both get a notification when it ends.\n\nLeaders set up pairings under [Admin, Mentoring](admin-reports-mentoring-audit.md).\n",
  "messages": "# Messages\n\n**Messages** is where the production team talks: a chat for each department, group chats, and direct messages between two people. It's there once a Platform Administrator switches it on under **Admin → Features**.\n\n## Finding it\n\n- **On a computer or tablet:** choose **Messages** in the side bar, under **Training**.\n- **On a phone:** choose **Messages** in the middle of the bottom bar.\n\nA number on Messages shows how many messages you haven't read. It updates about once a minute, and whenever you move to another page.\n\n## Your chats\n\nThe list shows your chats in three groups, each with its last message, the time, and how many are unread:\n\n- **Group chats**, such as one for everyone in the Academy.\n- **Department chats:** one for each department. You're in a department's chat if you're a member of the department, on one of its serving teams in Planning Center, or one of its leaders. Joining or leaving changes this straight away.\n- **Direct messages** between you and one other person.\n\nType in **Search chats** to find one by name or by what was said last.\n\n## Talking in a chat\n\nOpen a chat, write in the box at the bottom, and choose **Send**. On a computer, Enter sends too, and Shift and Enter starts a new line. New messages appear as they're sent, without refreshing, while the chat is open.\n\nLeaders have a **Leader** label beside their name.\n\nTo answer a particular message, choose the **…** beside it, then **Reply**. Your message shows what it's answering.\n\nIf you lose your connection, the chat says **You're offline**. What you write waits, and sends when you're back. If a message says **Not sent**, choose **Try again**.\n\n## Photos, GIFs and documents\n\nChoose the **+** beside the message box:\n\n- **Photo:** choose up to 4. They're made smaller on your phone before they're sent, which also removes where they were taken. The chat shows small versions; tap one to see it full size.\n- **GIF:** search GIPHY and tap one to send it. Only GIFs rated G appear. What you type in the search goes to GIPHY.\n- **Document:** a PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint or text file, up to 10 MB, in a message of its own. Others in the chat tap it to download it. Programs, and files that aren't what their name says, are refused.\n\nPhotos and documents are kept for 90 days, and messages for 3 years, unless a Platform Administrator changes them. After that the message stays, and says the photo or file is no longer available. Save anything you need to keep.\n\n## Starting a direct message\n\nChoose the pencil at the top of the list, find the person, and choose them. You can message anyone in the Academy.\n\nDirect messages are private between the two of you. Nobody else can read them, including leaders, the Production Director and Platform Administrators.\n\n## Group chats\n\nDepartment Leaders, the Production Director and Platform Administrators can make group chats. Choose the pencil, then **New group chat**:\n\n1. Give the group a name.\n2. Leave **Everyone in the Academy** on for a chat with everyone. New people join it automatically, and anyone who leaves the Academy leaves it. Or turn it off and choose people.\n3. Choose **Make the group**.\n\nEveryone in a group can write in it. The person who made it can rename it from the **…** at the top right of the chat.\n\n## Deleting a message\n\nChoose the **…** beside a message, then **Delete message**, and confirm. It's removed for everyone in the chat.\n\n- **You** can delete your own messages.\n- **A department's leaders** can delete any message in their department's chat. It then shows as **Message deleted by a leader**.\n- **In a group chat,** the person who made it, the Production Director and Platform Administrators can delete any message.\n\nNobody else can delete someone's direct messages. Every deletion goes in the audit log, with who deleted it and when. The log never keeps what the message said.\n\n## Being told about new messages\n\n**On your phone, alerts come straight away** when someone sends a message, if you've turned on alerts for your device. Each chat has one alert that updates as more arrive, for example \"4 new messages in Camera Operations\", so a busy chat doesn't buzz again and again: it waits at least 20 seconds before alerting you again. A direct message alert says who it's from, but never what they wrote.\n\nYou aren't alerted for your own messages, a chat you've muted, or a chat you have open on screen.\n\n**New messages are never emailed.** To hear about them away from the app, turn on alerts on your phone.\n\nUnder the bell, then **Settings**, the **Messages** group has a **Phone** button: turn it off to stop message alerts. New messages always show under the bell. Alerts come at any time of day, so use your phone's Do Not Disturb or mute a chat if you want quiet.\n\n## Earlier messages and who's in a chat\n\nA chat opens on its latest messages. To read further back, scroll to the top and choose **Show earlier messages**.\n\nTo see who's in a chat, choose the **…** at the top right, then **People in this chat**. Leaders have a **Leader** label.\n\n- **Department chats** follow the department: joining it, or being on one of its serving teams, puts you in.\n- **Everyone in the Academy** includes everyone, and new people join automatically.\n- **Group chats of chosen people:** the person who made the group can add people (find them under **Add someone**) and remove them. Anyone in the group can choose **Leave this group**. Someone who leaves or is removed can't read it any more. Adding, removing and leaving go in the audit log.\n\n## Quieter chats\n\nChoose the **…** at the top right of a chat, then **Mute notifications**, to stop being told about that chat. Muted chats don't add to the number on Messages.\n",
  "schedule": "# Schedule\n\n**Schedule** shows when you're serving and who with.\n\n## Reminders\n\nThe Academy reminds you if you haven't answered after two days, two days before you serve, and on the morning you serve. Planning Center sends its own reminders too, so you may get both. Between 8 pm and 8 am reminders wait until the morning.\n\n## Planning Center does the scheduling\n\nLeaders schedule in Planning Center, and the Academy reads from it every 15 minutes. So some things you do there, not here:\n\n- **Accept or decline** a request. Choose **Accept or decline in Planning Center**.\n- **Block out dates** you're away. Choose **Manage in Planning Center**.\n\nIf something you changed there doesn't show yet, wait up to 15 minutes. The page says when it last read from Planning Center.\n\n## What you'll see\n\n- **Today**, if you're serving today.\n- **Upcoming assignments:** each service, your position, and the time. Times are always Winnipeg time.\n- **Calendar:** your month at a glance, with a key to what each mark means. Tap a day to see it.\n- **Blocked dates** coming up.\n- **Who's serving with you** on each service. Choose it to see the team.\n- **Open checklist** takes you to that service's [Checklist](checklist.md).\n\n## Add it to your own calendar\n\nChoose **Add to your calendar (.ics)** to subscribe from your phone or computer's calendar app, so your assignments show up there too.\n\n## You aren't on the schedule yet?\n\nTeam managers add people to position teams in Planning Center when they're ready, and they schedule from those teams. Training shows beside your name as \"Trained\" or \"Still learning\", but it doesn't decide this. If you think you should be on a team, talk to your department leader.\n\n## If the Academy schedules instead\n\nAn administrator can switch scheduling from Planning Center to the Academy. It isn't switched on today. The page looks the same, with the calendar, the day you've chosen, upcoming assignments and blocked dates, and under the title it says **Scheduled in the Academy**. What changes:\n\n- **Answer here.** On the day, choose **Confirm** or **Decline**. If you decline, your scheduler is told, and the Academy asks a teammate who's free that day.\n- **Can't make it?** Choose **Ask for cover** on the day, with a note if you like. Teammates who are free are asked, and you stay scheduled until someone takes it.\n- **Can you cover?** and **Open spots:** cover a teammate who can't make it, or sign up for a place that still needs someone on a day you're free.\n- **Blockouts:** add days you can't serve (one day, a date range, part of a day, or repeating, such as every 1st Sunday, each with an optional reason your scheduler sees), and remove them. Here too are your **Calendar link**, which you add to Google, Apple or Outlook Calendar once so your serving stays up to date there (making a new link stops the old one), and **How I like to serve** (at most so many times a month, and whether to serve with someone in your household, or never at the same service).\n- **Files for your services:** stage plots and run sheets for services you've been asked to.\n\n**On a computer**, **Can you cover?** and **Open spots** are cards beside the calendar, and **Calendar link**, **How I like to serve** and **Files** open a panel on the right. **Manage blocked dates** opens the same panel.\n\n**On a phone**, three tabs sit under the title: **My schedule** (this page), **Open spots** and **Blockouts**. The other two open as a sheet from the bottom. In **Open spots**, each row is tagged **Cover** or **Open spot**, so you can tell them apart. Close a sheet by dragging its handle down, tapping outside it, or with **×**. On Android the back button closes it too.\n",
  "service-dashboard": "# The Service Dashboard: a guide for new people\n\n**Status:** being built in stages. This guide describes what was approved on 2026-11-02, and it gets updated every time the dashboard changes.\n\n**Working today:** the Service Dashboard page, the side bar link, one shared layout, editors, the layout editor, the app name and logo setting, and the tiles that come from Planning Center and the Academy: Run of service, Current item, Service timer, Who's serving, Checklist, Producer notes, and SOP shortcuts. The wall TV screen also works, and so does YouTube live once its setup is done (see the setup guide). To set up the connections, see the [Service Dashboard setup guide](../runbooks/service-dashboard.md). Slides, ProPresenter timers, Mixer levels, and Encoder health are built and show \"Not connected yet\" until the church bridge is set up. Items that have to be proven on real gear are listed at the end.\n\n## What it is\n\nThe Service Dashboard is one screen that shows the whole service while it runs: where we are in the plan, what's on the slides, and how the stream is doing.\n\nYou don't have to ask around or open six different apps. If you're serving, you can look up and see what's happening.\n\n**It only watches. It never controls anything.** Nothing you do on the dashboard can change a slide, move a fader, or stop the stream. You still run those from ProPresenter, the mixer, and the encoder, as you always have. If the dashboard stops working, the service carries on untouched.\n\n## Where to find it\n\n- **On a computer:** sign in to the Academy and choose **Service Dashboard** in the side bar.\n- **On the wall TV:** it's already there. The TV runs it by itself.\n- **On a phone or in the installed app:** it isn't available. A phone browser shows a message asking you to open it on a computer. Your training, schedule, checklist, and SOPs all still work on your phone.\n\nWho can open it follows Planning Center teams, the same way the Checklist does. If you're on a linked team, you have it. You don't need to finish any training first.\n\n## One layout for everyone\n\nService Producer, Audio, Graphics, Camera Director, and the wall TV all see the same screen. That means when someone says \"look at the run of service,\" everyone is looking at the same thing.\n\n## What each tile tells you\n\n| Tile                | What it shows                                                        | Where it comes from              |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| YouTube live        | **Live** or **Not live**                                             | YouTube                          |\n| Stream preview      | The YouTube live stream, a little behind the room. Off by default; an editor can add it | YouTube                          |\n| Current item        | What we're on, how long is left, and what's next                     | The plan and ProPresenter        |\n| Run of service      | Every item with its time, and the current one marked NOW             | Planning Center                  |\n| Slides              | The slide on screen and the next three                               | ProPresenter                     |\n| Service timer       | Time left in the whole service, and when it should end               | Worked out from the plan         |\n| ProPresenter timers | The timers running in ProPresenter                                   | ProPresenter                     |\n| Encoder health      | Whether the Magewell has a good input and is streaming                | Magewell Ultra Encode AIO        |\n| Who's serving       | Everyone on the team today, by position                              | Planning Center                  |\n| Checklist           | Today's tasks. Ticking one here is the same as ticking it in the app | The Academy Checklist            |\n| Producer notes      | Shared notes for this service                                        | Typed by the team                |\n| Mixer levels        | Signal levels for the channels the editors chose                     | The mixer                        |\n| SOP shortcuts       | Quick links to the procedures you need most                          | The Academy SOP library          |\n| Room loudness (SPL) | Switched off for now. See below                                      | A measurement mic, when we add it |\n\nEditors choose which tiles are on the layout, so you may not see all of these.\n\n### A few things that are easy to misread\n\n- **Current item goes by the plan's clock.** It works out where the service should be from the times in Planning Center, not what is really on screen. The Slides tile shows what is really on screen.\n- **The plan updates every 15 minutes,** so a late change in Planning Center can take that long to appear. The next two weeks of plans are read.\n\n- **The Slides tile may show words instead of pictures** if ProPresenter doesn't send a picture of a slide.\n- **Mixer levels are not room loudness.** They show the signal on each channel. The mixer can't measure how loud the room is. That takes a measurement mic or SMAART, which we don't have yet, so the Room loudness tile is built and left switched off.\n- **The stream preview runs behind the room.** That's normal for a livestream. Don't use it to judge timing.\n- **\"Trained\" and \"Still learning\" are information, not permission.** They show beside each name so you know who may need a hand. Team managers decide who serves.\n\n## What the messages mean\n\n| You see                     | What it means                                                                | What to do                                                       |\n| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Offline, last seen 10:12** | The tile lost its connection to the church gear. It shows the last reading   | Check that the bridge computer is on. The service isn't affected |\n| **Ready, switched off**     | The tile is built, but the gear it needs isn't connected                     | Nothing                                                          |\n\n## Changing the layout\n\nUp to three people can edit the layout. Everyone else can view it but can't change it.\n\nIf you're an editor, choose **Edit layout**. Then:\n\n- **Add a tile:** drag it in from the list.\n- **Move a tile:** drag it by its dots.\n- **Resize a tile:** drag its bottom-right corner.\n- **Remove a tile:** choose **Remove**.\n- **On a keyboard:** focus a tile, use the arrow keys to move it, Shift and the arrow keys to resize it, and Delete to remove it.\n\nChoose **Save layout** when you're done. Every computer and the wall TV pick up the change.\n\nRemember that the wall TV has to fit everything on one screen with no scrolling. The more tiles you add, the smaller the text gets, so check the TV after a big change.\n\n### Choosing mixer channels\n\nOn the Mixer levels tile, choose **Choose channels**, or go to **Service Dashboard setup**, then **Mixer channels**. Tick any channels, aux inputs, FX returns, mix buses, matrix outputs, or mains, use **Up** and **Down** to set the order, and choose **Save channels**. Until someone chooses, the tile shows the mains and the first eight channels. This only changes what the dashboard shows. It never touches the mixer.\n\n## The wall TV\n\nThe TV has no computer, mouse, or keyboard, and nobody signs in on it.\n\nOur TV is likely a Roku TV, and Roku has no proper web browser. So a small Android TV or Google TV stick plugs into the HDMI port and runs the dashboard full screen.\n\nThe TV is always in the black theme. On a computer, the dashboard follows the light and dark switch beside your name.\n\nOn the TV, checklists show totals, such as \"5 of 8\", and notes can be read but not edited.\n\n**To set up a TV, one time:**\n\n1. Plug in the stick and open the screen address, which is the Academy's address followed by `/screen`. The TV shows a 6-digit code.\n2. On your phone or computer, sign in and go to **Admin**, then **Service Dashboard setup**, then **Screens**. Enter the code.\n3. On the Roku TV, go to **Settings**, then **System**, **Power**, and **Power on**, and choose that HDMI input. The menu can differ by TV brand. This sends the TV back to the dashboard after a power cut.\n\nThe TV holds a view-only pass, not anyone's account. It can't tick checklists or edit notes. To disconnect a TV, choose **Remove** beside it in **Screens**, and it's signed out straight away.\n\n## How it gets its information\n\n**From the internet, with nothing to do each week:**\n\n- **Planning Center** provides the plan, the times, and who's serving. The Academy only reads from Planning Center and never changes it.\n- **YouTube** only needs our channel and an API key, because our stream is public. Nobody signs in. The Magewell goes live as usual, and the YouTube live tile says so. It checks from 30 minutes before the service until 30 minutes after, while a screen is open, so keep the wall TV or a booth computer on. It can take a couple of minutes to change to **Live**.\n\nThe dashboard doesn't count viewers. To see how many people are watching, check YouTube Studio or Facebook directly.\n\n**From the church network, through the bridge:**\n\nThe cloud can't reach the mixer, ProPresenter, or the Magewell directly. So a small program called the **bridge** runs on a church computer. It reads those three and sends the readings out to the Academy about once a second. It only connects outward, so no ports are opened on the church network.\n\nIf that computer is off, the tiles that depend on it go faded and say **Offline**. Everything else keeps working.\n\n## After the service\n\nChoose **After the service** at the top of the dashboard. It builds itself from what was recorded: how long each item ran against the plan. Use it to see where the service ran long.\n\n- **Actual times** come from Planning Center, and appear only when someone runs the plan with Services LIVE. Without that, an item says \"Not recorded\".\n\n## App name and logo\n\nPlatform administrators can change the app's name, short name, and logo under **Admin**, then **App name and logo**.\n\nThe built-in logo is **Signal Line**: a line that rises in three steps to a dot. **Original** brings it back at any time.\n\n- **A logo you upload or make on the page** replaces Signal Line everywhere in the app and on the installed app. **Signal Line mark** makes one from the mark on the colour you pick.\n- **A new name** keeps the Signal Line mark, but the full logo on the sign-in page becomes the mark with your new name beside it, since the built-in logo spells \"IFC Production Academy\".\n- **Link previews** (when someone shares a link to the Academy) always show Signal Line.\n\n- **New installs** get the change straight away.\n- **Android** picks it up by itself, but it can take days, and the name may not change in every case.\n- **iPhone** will most likely need the app removed and added again.\n- **Certificates already issued** keep the name they were issued under.\n- **The sign-in email and the offline page** keep the original name for now, because they are fixed files.\n\n## Words we use\n\n| Word          | Meaning                                                                       |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Tile          | One box on the dashboard                                                      |\n| Layout        | Which tiles appear and where                                                  |\n| Editor        | One of up to three people allowed to change the layout                        |\n| Bridge        | The small program on a church computer that reads the gear                    |\n| Screen        | A TV connected by code, with a view-only pass                                 |\n| Scribble strip | The small name display above each channel on the mixer                       |\n| SPL           | Sound pressure level: how loud the room actually is                           |\n\n## Still to be proven\n\nThese were approved knowing they need a real test. This guide will change once they're done.\n\n- **ProPresenter:** the exact way to read slide status, and whether it can send pictures of the next three slides. If it only sends text, those tiles show the words.\n- **X32:** the exact meter format, and reading channel names from the scribble strips.\n- **Magewell:** whether its web API needs a sign-in.\n- **YouTube:** how soon after going live the YouTube live tile changes, on our real channel.\n- **The TV stick:** that it reopens the dashboard by itself after a power cut.\n- **Services LIVE times:** that they arrive as expected, the first time someone runs a plan live.\n- **Text size on the TV:** whether it can be read from where people stand.\n- **If the mixer changes:** the Allen & Heath dLive has a documented connection, but its meter access is unconfirmed. We found no documented way to read meters on the Waves LV1 Classic, so that tile would likely go away.\n",
  "sops": "# SOPs\n\nSOPs are the team's written procedures: how to start the stream, how to set up a camera, what to do when something goes wrong.\n\n## Finding one\n\nOpen **SOPs** in the side bar and start typing to search. On a phone with Messages switched on, choose **Serve** on the bottom bar, then **SOPs** at the top of the checklist. Results update as you type, and search looks in titles, summaries, and categories. You can also choose a category, see what was **Recently updated**, or narrow it by department. Some SOPs are for **All of production** rather than one department.\n\nThe list shows when each SOP was last reviewed, and marks any that are due for review. Open an SOP to see its summary and owner.\n\n## Reading one\n\nOpen an SOP to read its steps, and open its file if it has one, usually a PDF. Some have both, such as written steps and a wiring diagram.\n\n- **Only the current version can be opened,** so nobody follows an old procedure by mistake. Version history shows what changed and when.\n- **\"Due for review\"** means nobody has confirmed it's still right lately. It's probably fine, but check with its owner if something looks off.\n- **\"Archived\"** means it's no longer in use. It's kept for the record.\n\n## Where else you'll see them\n\nLessons can attach SOPs, and always show the current version. The [Service Dashboard](service-dashboard.md) can also have shortcuts to them.\n\nLeaders look after these under [Admin, SOP library](admin-sops.md).\n",
  "training": "# Training\n\n**Training** is your home in the Academy. From the top:\n\n- **A greeting**, then, if you're on the schedule, a bold card for your next service: when, your position, and buttons for **Open checklist** and **Schedule**. If you haven't replied yet, it asks you to **Reply on Schedule**, with the **Checklist** beside it.\n- **Pick up where you left off:** the one thing to do next, with a button to carry on.\n- **Coming up:** anything else, such as courses a leader assigned, other services this week, practicals, and other courses you've started.\n- **Shortcuts** on a phone: Checklist, Schedule, SOPs, and Mentoring.\n- **Your departments**, in a row you can scroll sideways. Each shows how many of its lessons you've marked complete.\n- **Recently watched:** the lessons you opened lately. It shows roughly how much of a video you watched, as a reminder only, and a tick once you've marked a lesson **Completed**.\n- **Keep exploring:** a few courses to try, in a row you can scroll sideways.\n\nIn a row that scrolls sideways, the next card peeks in at the edge, and the dots underneath show where you are. On a tablet or computer, use the arrow buttons beside the heading.\n\nOn a wide screen, **This month** (your services) sits beside your next service. Choose one to do what's next: if it says **Please reply**, it opens Schedule; otherwise it opens that service's checklist on your position. Your notifications are under the bell.\n\n**Getting started** shows instead of the plan when you're new: add your name, start your first course, join a department, and get the app.\n\n The side bar has **Courses** and **Explore**. On a phone, choose **Courses** on the bottom bar, then switch between **Courses** and **Explore** at the top.\n\n## Anyone can learn anything\n\nEvery open department's courses are open to you. You don't need anyone's permission to start one, and you can take a course simply because you're curious.\n\n**Joining a department is a separate thing.** It means you're part of that department's team, so its leader knows you and can plan around you. To join, choose **Request to join** on a department. A leader reviews each request, and some departments are by invitation only.\n\n## The Library\n\n- **Courses** lists every course, with search and filters for category, level, and topic. At the top, **Browse by department** is a row you can scroll sideways: tap a department to see only its courses, and tap it again to see them all. On a phone, courses come in rows you scroll sideways: **Continue** for the ones you've started, then a row for each category. **See all** shows one category's courses on their own. Searching or choosing a filter shows the matching courses together.\n- **Explore** finds courses and single videos, whichever department they're in. It opens on its search and filters for level, category, and topic. Once you search or choose one, matching courses come first, with single videos below them. Opening a video takes you to that lesson in its course, and it counts there like any other lesson.\n\n## General categories\n\n**General categories**, such as General and Leadership, are courses for everyone on the production team, whichever department you serve in. There's nothing to join: open a topic and start a course. Finishing one earns a certificate, but it doesn't count towards being Approved to Serve.\n\n## Assigned to you\n\nIf a leader asks you to take a course, it shows on Training until you finish it, marked **Assigned by a leader**. Go at your own pace. It doesn't stop you doing anything else, and it doesn't put you on a team.\n\nA leader can give it a due date. The notification says when, and you're told if the date changes or is taken off. A due date is a date to aim for: missing it never stops you doing anything.\n\n## Start here\n\nSome courses, such as A Guide to Serving, are marked **Start here**. The ones you haven't finished show in your plan on Training, and they're listed under **Before you start** on each department's page. They're a good place to begin, but nothing is locked until you've done them.\n\nChoosing a course anywhere opens its summary, with a button to start or continue. To jump straight back in, use **Continue** under **Pick up where you left off** on Training.\n\n## How a department is set out\n\nEach department has three levels, one step at a time. Each level has courses, and each course has lessons, and usually a quiz and a practical assessment.\n\nA department's page opens with its colour, name and what it's for, with its course covers fanned out beside them. Beside or below that, **Up next** shows the cover of the course to carry on with, how much of the department you've done, and a **Continue** button. On a phone, once you scroll past it, a slim **Up next** bar above the tabs keeps Continue in reach.\n\n**Your path** shows the levels in order, each with a ring of the lessons you've done. The level you're working on opens first. Choose any level to open or close it and see its courses, each with its own cover.\n\n**What each role still needs** lists each serving role in the department as a short checklist: being a member, then each practical skill the role needs, ticked once you've passed it. Courses aren't on it, because finishing them never approves you to serve on its own.\n\nOn Training, each department you belong to has a bar showing how many of its lessons you've marked complete, with the count beside it, such as **3 of 10 lessons**. When a new level opens, the bar drops because there are more lessons to do, and what you've finished still counts. A full bar means the lessons so far are complete. It doesn't mean you're approved to serve.\n\n| Label on a department | What it means |\n| --- | --- |\n| **See courses** | It's open. Go ahead. |\n| **Coming soon** | You can see what's planned, but training opens when the curriculum is ready. |\n| **Join** | Anyone can join this department straight away. |\n| **By invitation** | Ask the department leader for an invite to join. |\n| **Request sent** | A department leader will review it. |\n\n## Taking a course\n\nOpen a course to see its cover, how long it takes, and what it covers. Choose **Start the course**, or **Continue** to pick up where you left off.\n\nOn the course page, **About** says what you'll learn and **Quiz results** lists the quizzes you've taken. The **Lessons** panel shows your progress and every lesson in order, with each quiz and practical in its place. On a phone, the lessons come first.\n\n### Starting over\n\nOpen a course you've started, choose **Actions** at the top right, then **Start over** to begin it again from the first lesson. The app warns you first and asks you to confirm.\n\nIt clears your completed lessons, your place in each video, and your quiz results for that course, so you'll retake its quizzes. Your certificate, any practical you've passed, and whether you're approved to serve all stay as they are. Leaders' reports show your new progress. You can't undo it yourself, but your old results are kept on record.\n\n### Lessons\n\nA lesson may have a video, what you'll learn, and attached SOPs. When you've done it, mark it **Completed** yourself. The Academy never claims a video was fully watched, so it's on your honour.\n\nSome lessons are optional. The course outline marks them. Only the ones needed **To finish the course** count.\n\n### Quizzes\n\nThe app scores your answers against the pass mark the department set. That's usually 80%, with 3 attempts, though a department can change either.\n\n- If you don't pass, go back over the lessons and try again.\n- If you've used all your attempts, talk to your trainer or leader. They can give you another one.\n- You can leave and come back: an unfinished attempt waits for you under **Resume the quiz**.\n\nPassing a quiz counts towards the course. It doesn't approve you to serve.\n\n### Practical assessments\n\nThis is where someone watches you do the job.\n\n1. Open the practical, and read **What the trainer watches for**.\n2. When you're ready, choose **Request assessment**. A trainer will arrange a time with you.\n3. They tick what they saw on the checklist. You don't get a score from them: the app works out the result from the department's pass rule.\n\nThe result is **Pass** or **Not yet**. \"Not yet\" has no penalty and no waiting period. Practise, and ask again when you're ready.\n\nA pass grants the competency. If that was the last one a role needs, you're **Approved to Serve** in that role automatically.\n\n## Certificates\n\nWhen you finish a course, its certificate appears on the course and on your profile, ready to download. Each one has a reference and a QR code, so anyone can check it's real. If a certificate was issued by mistake, a leader can withdraw it. It then says so, and the record is kept. You get a notification if one of yours is withdrawn.\n\n## Your next steps\n\nIf you're being welcomed onto the team through an onboarding pathway, **Your next steps** shows at the top of Training: where you are, who your contact is, and what's next. Some steps move on by themselves when you finish a course.\n\nFinishing courses never decides on its own whether you serve. A team leader adds you to a team when you're ready.\n\n## Lesson content changes\n\nLeaders update courses over time. What you've already earned stays linked to the version you earned it on, so an update never takes away your progress or certificate.\n",
  "video-and-graphics-approvals": "# Video and graphics approvals\n\nEverything shown during a livestream is checked for copyright first. This replaces the old JotForm\n\"Video / Graphics Approval Form\". It's switched on by an admin once Quality control and the emails\nare set up; until then the form says requests are paused.\n\n## Asking to show a video or graphic\n\nAnyone can ask, with no account, at **/media-request** (for example\n`https://your-academy-address/media-request`). Share that link where the JotForm link used to be.\n\n1. Enter your name and email. The answer comes to that email.\n2. Choose **A video** or **A graphic**.\n   - A video is always a link: YouTube, Vimeo or Google Drive. Make sure anyone with the link can\n     view it.\n   - A graphic can be uploaded (PNG, JPEG, WebP or PDF, up to 20 MB) or linked.\n3. Choose the service from the Planning Center schedule, or **Another date…** for an event that\n   isn't on it.\n4. Say what it's for, such as \"a missions update before the offering\", and press **Send request**.\n\n**The deadline.** Requests need to arrive a set number of days before the service: 5 days unless an\nadmin changes it, counted as calendar days or business days (Monday to Friday). If you're cutting it\nclose, the form tells you. A late request is still sent, marked Late, and may not be checked in\ntime. An admin can choose to refuse late requests instead.\n\nYou can send up to 5 requests a day from one email address.\n\n## Checking on your request\n\nStraight away you get a \"We got your request\" email with a **See its progress** link. That page\nshows:\n\n- **Sent**, with the time.\n- **With Quality control**, who look at every request first.\n- **Copyright review, if needed.** Only some requests need it.\n- **Decision**, approved or not, with any note from the team.\n\nOnly people with your email's link can see it. Keep the email: the link is the only way back.\n\n## The answer\n\nYou get an email when it's decided: **Approved**, or **Not approved** with what would help. If it\nwasn't approved, the email and the progress page have a **Send a new version** button.\n\n## For Quality control\n\nQuality control doesn't need an Academy account. Each new request is emailed to everyone on the\nQuality control list with a **Review it** button. That link is just for you: it opens your list of\nopen requests and works for 30 days, as long as you're on the list. Each new email brings a fresh\nlink.\n\n1. Open a request. Your name goes on it, so two people don't check the same one.\n2. Watch the video or open the graphic, and tick the checks if they help. They never decide for\n   you.\n3. Choose **Where it goes in the service** from the Planning Center order of service, so the team\n   knows when it plays. Leave it as **Not placed yet** if the plan isn't ready.\n4. Then choose what happens next:\n   - **Approve** or **Not approved**, with a note. The requester is emailed straight away. If you\n     decline, say what would help.\n   - **Copyright review**, if the rights need a closer look. Pick one or more reviewers and write\n     what they should check, such as \"Is the background song covered by our streaming licence?\"\n\nRequests decided in the last 30 days stay on your list underneath.\n\n## For copyright reviewers\n\nQuality control only sends a request for a copyright review when it needs one. The first reviewer\nto decide closes it. The decision goes straight to the requester, and Quality control gets a copy.\n\n- **In the Academy.** If you have the Copyright reviewer role, **Copyright** appears in your side\n  bar on a computer or tablet, with a count of what's waiting. The email's **Review it** button\n  opens the same request, after you sign in.\n- **Outside the Academy.** You don't need an account. The email's **Review it** link opens that one\n  request. It stops working once someone decides, or after 14 days.\n\nEither way, you see Quality control's question, the video or graphic, and the details. Choose\n**Approve** or **Not approved** and write a note for the requester. Need to ask something first?\nReply to the email.\n\n## Words we use\n\n| Word            | Meaning                                                                       |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Quality control | The team that checks every request first                                      |\n| Copyright review | A closer look at the rights, only when Quality control asks for one          |\n| Late            | Sent with less notice than the deadline                                       |\n| Reference       | The request's number, such as CR-0142, to quote if you have a question        |\n",
  "runbook-backups": "# Backups, the restore test, and the weekly summary\n\nSupabase Free keeps no backups, so the Academy makes its own (SPEC §9 \"Backup design\"):\n\n- **Weekly** (`.github/workflows/backup.yml`, Sundays 08:00 UTC; weekly since 2026-11-01): the whole database (`pg_dump`\n  custom format, version 17 like Supabase) and every stored SOP file, encrypted with `age` on\n  the runner, uploaded with SHA-256 checksums to the **IFC Academy Backups** Shared Drive. Old\n  copies are deleted: the last 30 are kept (about 7 months).\n- **Monthly** (`restore-test.yml`, the 3rd, 10:00 UTC): downloads the newest backup, checks its\n  checksums, decrypts it, restores it into a throwaway Postgres 17 container that uses\n  Supabase's own image, and compares row counts with the counts taken when it was made.\n- **Weekly** (`weekly-summary.yml`, Mondays 14:00 UTC): emails when each job last succeeded and\n  commits `status/last-run.json`, which stops GitHub disabling scheduled jobs after 60 quiet days.\n  A keep-alive with no success for two days, or a backup with none for eight, counts as an incident\n  and alerts the admins. It also lists the notification emails, chat cleanup, and the Planning\n  Center sync (late after a day without a good run).\n\n**Chat photos and documents are left out** (the `chat-files` bucket, `SKIPPED_BUCKETS` in\n`lib/domain/backup.ts`): they're removed after 90 days anyway, and would make every copy larger.\nMessage text is in the database, so it is backed up. After a restore, messages say their photo\nor file is no longer available.\n\nThe weekly summary also reports how much of the Supabase free plan is used (database and stored\nfiles, in bytes) and warns at 70%, with what to change on **Admin → Features**.\n\nNone of it logs data: only status, timings, sizes, and file counts. Nothing is ever uploaded as a\nworkflow artifact. Backups and restore tests are **off until the repository variable\n`BACKUPS_ENABLED` is `true`**; until then scheduled runs show as skipped.\n\n## One-time setup\n\n### 1. The encryption keys\n\nOn a trusted computer, install [age](https://github.com/FiloSottile/age) and make two keys:\n\n```sh\nage-keygen -o academy-backup.key        # the real key: keep it offline\nage-keygen -o academy-restore-test.key  # only for the monthly restore test\n```\n\n- **`academy-backup.key`** decrypts every backup. Store it offline (for example a password\n  manager entry or printed in a safe) with the Production Director and the Platform Admin. Never\n  put it in Google Drive or GitHub.\n- **`academy-restore-test.key`** lets the monthly job prove a backup restores. It lives in GitHub\n  secrets, so anyone who can change this repository's workflows could use it to decrypt a backup\n  they also downloaded from the Shared Drive. If that trade-off isn't acceptable, leave it out:\n  backups still run, and you restore-test by hand each month with the offline key instead.\n\nEach key file prints its public key (`age1…`). Both public keys go into `AGE_RECIPIENTS`.\n\n### 2. The Shared Drive, service account, and keyless sign-in\n\nNew Google Cloud organizations block service-account key files\n(`iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation`), and keyless is safer anyway. The jobs use **Workload\nIdentity Federation**: GitHub proves to Google that it's this repository's job on `main`, and\nGoogle returns a short-lived Drive token. There's no key to leak.\n\n1. In IFC's Google Workspace, create a **Shared Drive** named \"IFC Academy Backups\". Limit members\n   to the Production Director and the Platform Admin. Its folder ID is the last part of its address.\n2. In Google Cloud, in the backup project, enable the **Google Drive API**, **IAM Service Account\n   Credentials API**, and **Security Token Service API**.\n3. Create a **service account** (`academy-backup`), with no roles and no keys.\n4. **IAM & Admin → Workload Identity Federation → Create pool**: name and ID `github`. Add a\n   provider:\n   - **OpenID Connect (OIDC)**, name and ID `github-actions`\n   - Issuer URL `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`, audiences: default\n   - Attribute mapping: `google.subject` = `assertion.sub`, `attribute.repository` =\n     `assertion.repository`, `attribute.ref` = `assertion.ref`\n   - Attribute condition, so only this repository's `main` gets in:\n     `assertion.repository == \"gboyegaadeojo/IFC-Production-Academy\" && assertion.ref == \"refs/heads/main\"`\n5. On the pool, **Grant access → Grant access using service account impersonation**: choose\n   `academy-backup`, attribute `repository`, value `gboyegaadeojo/IFC-Production-Academy`. Skip\n   the configuration file download.\n6. Add the service account's email to the Shared Drive as a **Content manager** (no notification).\n7. Note the provider's resource name,\n   `projects/<project number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/github-actions`.\n\n### 3. The database connection string\n\nIn Supabase → Connect → **Session pooler** (IPv4), copy the connection string and put in the\ndatabase password. Use the session pooler: GitHub's runners can't reach Supabase's direct\n(IPv6) address. The same secret lets every deploy apply new database migrations before the app\ngoes live; without it, deploys warn that migrations weren't applied.\n\n### 4. Secrets and the variable\n\n```sh\ngh secret set SUPABASE_DB_URL               # the connection string from step 3\ngh secret set SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY     # Supabase → Project Settings → API keys\ngh secret set AGE_RECIPIENTS                # both public keys, separated by a space\ngh secret set AGE_RESTORE_IDENTITY < academy-restore-test.key   # optional, see step 1\ngh secret set GDRIVE_FOLDER_ID\ngh variable set GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER --body \"projects/<number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/github-actions\"\ngh variable set GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT --body \"academy-backup@<project>.iam.gserviceaccount.com\"\ngh variable set BACKUPS_ENABLED --body true\n```\n\nThe two `GCP_` values are identifiers, not secrets: without this repository's `main` branch they\nopen nothing. (Where key files are allowed, a `GDRIVE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` secret still works\ninstead.)\n\n`SUPABASE_URL`, `RESEND_API_KEY`, `ALERT_EMAILS`, and `ALERT_FROM` are shared with the other jobs.\nThen delete `academy-restore-test.key` from the computer.\n\n### 5. Check it\n\nRun **Backup** by hand (Actions → Backup → Run workflow). The log should show sizes and\n\"Retention: … kept, 0 deleted\", and three files should appear in the Shared Drive. Then run\n**Restore test** by hand and look for \"Restore test: pass\".\n\n## Restoring for real\n\nTarget: back online within 4 hours, losing at most a week of data.\n\n1. Download the newest `academy-db-….dump.age`, `academy-files-….zip.age`, and\n   `academy-….sha256` from the Shared Drive, and check them: `sha256sum -c academy-….sha256`.\n2. Decrypt with the offline key:\n   `age -d -i academy-backup.key -o db.dump academy-db-….dump.age` (and the same for the files).\n3. Create a fresh Supabase project (or reset the existing one), then restore:\n   `pg_restore --no-owner --no-privileges -d \"<session pooler connection string>\" db.dump`.\n   Supabase's own schemas already exist, so \"already exists\" messages are expected.\n4. Re-upload the files: unzip `files.zip`; each top-level folder is a Storage bucket. Upload them\n   to the same paths (for example with the Supabase CLI `supabase storage cp -r`). Chat files\n   aren't in the backup; the next run of `chat-cleanup.yml` marks their messages \"no longer\n   available\".\n5. Point the app at the project: update `SUPABASE_URL` and `SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` in GitHub\n   and run the deploy. Check you can sign in and that a course and an SOP open.\n6. Delete every decrypted copy.\n",
  "runbook-messaging": "# Messaging setup\n\nHow team messaging is switched on and set up (the team messaging decision of 8 November 2026). The guide for people using it is [Messages](../guides/messages.md).\n\n## Switching it on\n\nA Platform Administrator turns on **Messages** under **Admin → Features**. While it's off, nobody sees Messages, and the database refuses to send or show anything. Switching it is recorded in the audit log.\n\n## GIFs (GIPHY)\n\nGIF search uses GIPHY's free plan, through the Academy's server, rated G only.\n\n1. Sign in at developers.giphy.com and choose **Create an App**, then **API**, platform **Web**. Name it after the Academy.\n2. Copy the API key it shows.\n3. In Cloudflare, open **Workers & Pages**, the Academy's worker, **Settings → Variables and Secrets**, and add a **Secret** named `GIPHY_API_KEY`. Never put the key in the repository, an issue, or a chat.\n\nWithout the key, the GIF choice says GIFs aren't set up yet, and everything else works. A new GIPHY key allows 100 searches an hour. The app waits until typing stops and keeps results for five minutes, which is plenty for the team. Sent GIFs are GIPHY's address, not a file, so they use no storage.\n\n## Photos and documents\n\n- **Where they're kept:** the private `chat-files` bucket in Supabase Storage. Each file sits under its chat's id, and only people who can read that chat can upload into it or open it. Links last one minute.\n- **Photos:** made smaller in the browser to at most 1,600 px (WebP, or JPEG on older Safari), with a 320 px thumbnail. Chats load thumbnails; the full photo loads only when tapped.\n- **Documents:** PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint and plain text, up to 10 MB, checked by name and by their first bytes in the browser and again on the server. They open as downloads.\n- **How long:** 90 days for photos and documents, and 3 years for message text, unless a Platform Administrator changes them on **Admin → Features** (30 to 365 days, and 1 to 10 years). Changes go in the audit log.\n\n## The daily cleanup\n\n`.github/workflows/chat-cleanup.yml` runs every day at 09:30 UTC, and can be started by hand from the Actions tab. With the service role (an Actions secret only), it:\n\n1. removes stored photos and documents older than the limit, and those of deleted messages, and marks them removed, so their messages say the photo or file is no longer available;\n2. removes messages older than the text limit, once their files have gone.\n\nIt logs counts only. If it fails, the admins get an email. A missed day does no harm: the next run catches up.\n\nChat photos and documents are left out of the weekly backup ([backups](backups.md)); message text is backed up with the database.\n\n## Free plan budget\n\nSupabase Free has 500 MB of database and 1 GB of file storage, shared with SOP files, and 5 GB of downloads a month. A photo with its thumbnail is about 300 KB, so the 90-day cleanup keeps chats well inside the limit unless the team shares more than about 30 photos a day. The weekly summary shows how much is used and warns at 70%, saying what to change on Admin → Features.\n",
  "runbook-monitoring": "# Monitoring\n\nSentry tells you when the app breaks. PostHog counts which pages get used. Both are free tiers, and\nboth are off until you set their keys below. While they're off, the browser doesn't download either\none.\n\n## Turn them on\n\nAdd these under GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → **Variables**, then run the\nDeploy workflow. They're variables because both values reach the browser, so they can't be secret.\n\n| Variable | Where it comes from |\n| --- | --- |\n| `SENTRY_DSN` | Sentry → create a Next.js project → Project Settings → Client Keys (DSN) |\n| `POSTHOG_KEY` | PostHog → Project Settings → Project API key |\n| `POSTHOG_HOST` | Only for PostHog's EU region: `https://eu.i.posthog.com`. Leave it out for the US region |\n\nIn PostHog, also turn on **Discard client IP data** under Project Settings.\n\n## Where it's wired\n\n- **Browser:** `instrumentation-client.ts` loads Sentry and PostHog in a quiet moment and counts page views as you move between pages. The page error screen (`app/global-error.tsx`) hands its error to it.\n- **Server:** the Worker (`workers/entry.ts`) sets Sentry up and reports the app's server errors, which `instrumentation.ts` passes to it. Don't import Sentry or PostHog anywhere else in the app: each copy adds megabytes to what a cold start loads.\n\n## What gets sent\n\n**Sentry** receives the error, where in the code it happened, the browser, and the page's address.\nStack traces from the browser are minified, because source maps aren't uploaded.\n\n**PostHog** receives anonymous page views, counted per device, and no other kind of event. It\ndoesn't record sessions, clicks, or page speed, and it isn't told who anyone is, so it can't show\none person's activity. Training numbers such as\ncompletion rates come from the Academy's own reports.\n\n## What never gets sent\n\n`lib/domain/monitoring.ts` removes these before anything leaves the app:\n\n- names, email addresses, cookies, request headers, and request bodies\n- query strings, which can hold live room tickets and sign-in codes\n- invite tokens and certificate references in addresses\n- IDs, which become `[id]`\n- quoted values in error text, which is where database errors put names\n- page titles, which can name a person or course\n\nAddresses are scrubbed at any depth, because both tools repeat them in fields of their own.\n\nIf you add a page whose address holds a secret, add it to `PRIVATE_PATHS` in that file. After any\nchange to the monitoring code or a library upgrade, repeat the check below with a fake invite link,\nsuch as `/invite/fake-token?code=fake`, and search what's sent for those values. PostHog ignores\nautomated browsers, so an automated test needs to hide that it's one.\n\n## Check it works\n\n1. **Sentry:** after deploying, open the app, then run\n   `setTimeout(() => { throw new Error(\"Monitoring test\"); })` in the browser's console. It should\n   appear in Sentry within a minute.\n2. **PostHog:** open a few pages, then look under Activity. Open an event and check that its address\n   has no query string or ID.\n\n## When a job or a deploy fails\n\nEvery scheduled job, and the deploy, emails G and the Platform Admin through Resend\n(`.github/actions/notify-failure`). Since 2026-11-11 that's once per problem: the first failed run\nemails, later failed runs don't, and the first good run after them emails \"is working again\". The\nweekly summary still emails every week it finds a problem.\n\nAfter every deploy, `scripts/check-live.ts` checks the live site: the new version is being served,\npublic pages load, private pages send signed-out visitors to sign-in, and the database API answers\nthe keep-alive and the Planning Center sync's check. A failure fails the deploy job and emails as\nabove. It logs status codes and timings only.\n\nThe weekly summary also says if a job is late (the Planning Center sync after a day without a good\nrun), and if Manitoba's time rules seem to have changed (see\n`docs/decisions/2026-11-10-winnipeg-fixed-offset.md`).\n",
  "runbook-nas-media": "# Church video library (UniFi NAS)\n\nLessons can play training videos stored on the church's UniFi NAS, as well as unlisted YouTube\nvideos (decided 2026-10-30). This page is for whoever sets it up at IFC. It contains no hostnames,\naddresses, or credentials, and none should ever be added to this repository.\n\n## How it works\n\n```\nVolunteer's browser\n  → the Academy (Cloudflare Worker): checks they're signed in and may read the lesson\n  → Cloudflare Tunnel hostname, protected by Cloudflare Access (service token only)\n  → cloudflared and a read-only file server on a small computer at church\n  → the NAS training-videos share, mounted read-only\n```\n\n- The browser only ever sees `/media/lessons/<lesson id>` on the Academy. It never sees the NAS,\n  the tunnel hostname, or a file path. The link can't be shared: it plays only for someone signed\n  in who may read that lesson, which makes it suitable for restricted material (SRS-SOP-006).\n- Every request is checked, including each part of the video the player asks for while seeking.\n- Until it's set up, leaders can still add library file paths to lessons. Learners see \"The church\n  video library isn't connected yet\".\n\n## What you need\n\n- **A small always-on computer on the church network**, such as a Raspberry Pi 4 or 5, an old\n  mini PC, or any Docker host. The UniFi NAS may not be able to run the tunnel itself.\n- **A NAS account just for this**, with read-only access to one share or folder that holds only\n  the exported training videos. Don't share the whole NAS.\n- The church's **Cloudflare account** (the one that hosts the Academy). Tunnel and Access are on\n  the free plan (Access is free for up to 50 users; this uses one service token).\n\n## Setup\n\n1. **Export the videos for the web.** Use MP4 with H.264 video and AAC audio, about 720p at 2–3\n   Mbit/s, and turn on \"fast start\" (web optimised) so playback begins before the whole file\n   arrives. WebM also works. Use simple file names: letters, numbers, spaces, `.`, `_`, `-`.\n   Organise by department and level, for example `camera/level-1/framing.mp4`.\n2. **Mount the share read-only** on the small computer (SMB or NFS), for example at\n   `/srv/training-videos`.\n3. **Run a read-only file server on localhost only**, such as Caddy:\n\n   ```\n   :8080 {\n     bind 127.0.0.1\n     root * /srv/training-videos\n     file_server        # no \"browse\": directory listings stay off\n   }\n   ```\n\n4. **Create a Cloudflare Tunnel** (Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels) and install `cloudflared` on\n   the computer as a service. Add a public hostname on the church's domain that points to\n   `http://127.0.0.1:8080`.\n5. **Protect the hostname with Cloudflare Access** (Zero Trust → Access → Applications →\n   Self-hosted). Add one policy, with action **Service Auth**, that allows only a new service token\n   named for the Academy. Don't add any email or \"everyone\" rules. Copy the token's client ID and\n   secret once; Cloudflare doesn't show the secret again.\n6. **Give the Academy the three secrets** (Workers → the Academy → Settings → Variables and\n   Secrets, as type Secret, or `wrangler secret put`):\n   - `MEDIA_ORIGIN_URL`: `https://` plus the tunnel hostname.\n   - `MEDIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID` and `MEDIA_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET`: from step 5.\n\n   Don't put them in GitHub, an issue, a chat, or this repository.\n7. **Check it.**\n   - Add a library file to a test lesson, publish it, and play it as a member, seeking back and\n     forward.\n   - Then open the same lesson address while signed out, and as someone outside the department:\n     both must fail.\n   - Opening the tunnel hostname directly in a browser must show the Access block page.\n\n## Things to know\n\n- **Bandwidth.** Every viewer streams from the church's internet connection, at about 2.5 Mbit/s\n  per viewer. The church has a 1 Gbit/s upload (confirmed 2026-10-31), so the line is not the\n  limit: the small computer is. Use wired Ethernet (gigabit), not Wi-Fi, between it, the NAS, and\n  the router. If the connection changes, keep popular, non-sensitive videos on unlisted YouTube.\n- **Downtime.** If the small computer, the NAS, or the church internet is down, library videos\n  show \"The church video library isn't reachable right now\" and the lesson text still works.\n- **Cloudflare's terms.** Check Cloudflare's current terms for serving video through Workers and\n  Tunnel before go-live. Training volume is small, but this needs confirming, not assuming.\n- **Rotating the token.** Create a new service token, add it to the Access policy, update the two\n  secrets, then delete the old token.\n- **Logs** record only status codes and timings, never file paths, people, or tokens.\n",
  "runbook-notifications": "# Notification emails\n\nThe Academy writes a notification when something happens for a person: joining a department,\nbeing scheduled, a practical assessment result, a mentoring pairing, or an SOP they own coming\ndue for review. Everyone sees theirs in the app under **Notifications** (the bell beside their name), and\nchooses which kinds reach their phone and their email.\n\n## What they look like\n\nEvery email uses the Calm design in `lib/domain/email-design.ts`: the app's current logo (served at\n`/brand/icon`, so a new logo shows even in emails already sent), the title as the subject, the\nmessage as the inbox preview, a details box where it applies, and one button named for what it does\n(`emailButtonLabel`).\n\nThe details box (when, role and service; a course and its length; due and expiry dates) and a\nshorter line for phone alerts are looked up as each notification is written, from what its dedupe\nkey names (`private.add_notification_details()`). If nothing is found, the email has no box and the\nalert uses the message. Sign-in emails use the same design, sent by the app.\n\n## Sign-in emails\n\nSupabase makes the one-time sign-in token, then calls the app's `/api/auth/send-email` through\nits **Send Email hook**. The app checks Supabase's signature, builds the email\n(`lib/domain/auth-email.ts`), and sends it through Resend. So sign-in emails have the same design\nas everything else, and nothing is pasted into Supabase.\n\n- Secrets: `SEND_EMAIL_HOOK_SECRET` (GitHub Actions), plus `RESEND_API_KEY` and `NOTIFY_FROM`.\n  The deploy copies all three into Cloudflare.\n- The hook: Supabase → Authentication → Hooks → Send Email, type HTTPS, URL\n  `<site>/api/auth/send-email`, with the same secret.\n- If the app or Resend can't send, Supabase shows the person an error and nothing is sent, so they\n  can try again or use Planning Center sign-in.\n- **Local development** doesn't use the hook: Supabase sends with\n  `supabase/templates/magic-link.html` into Mailpit, as before.\n- **To switch it off,** turn the hook off in Supabase. Supabase then sends with its own template.\n\n## Which way each one travels\n\nSet in `private.route_notification()`, and decided in `docs/decisions/2026-11-05-alerts.md`:\n\n- **In the app:** everything.\n- **App only:** a certificate ready, a new course, and an updated SOP.\n- **Phone alert:** everything else, on devices with alerts on, unless the person turned that kind off.\n- **Email:** if the person has no alerts on a phone, or turned that kind off for their phone, and as\n  they've chosen. A moved or cancelled service comes by email as well as by alert.\n\n### Messages: instant phone alerts (ADD-MSG-06)\n\nMessage alerts don't wait for the job. When someone sends a message, the Worker replies first, then\n(with `after`, so sending is never slower) calls `claim_message_alerts()` with the service role and\nsends the alerts with the VAPID keys. Both are Cloudflare secrets already set for other alerts\n(`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`, and the public key `NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`).\n\n- **Who:** people who can read the chat, have alerts on a device, haven't muted it, haven't read it\n  in the last 30 seconds (an open chat refreshes this every 15), and have **Messages** on for their\n  phone. Not the sender.\n- **How often:** one alert per chat, with the same tag, so a new one replaces the last on the device\n  (\"4 new messages in Camera Operations\"), and at most one every 20 seconds per person per chat.\n- **Privacy:** a direct message alert says who it's from, never the words. Group and department chat\n  alerts show the start of the message.\n- **Rules that still apply:** the launch switch (on since 2026-11-09) and nothing while messaging\n  is off. There are no quiet hours (removed 2026-11-09).\n- **Logs:** counts only (\"Message alerts: 3 sent, 0 devices gone, 0 failed\"), in the Worker's logs.\n\nThe 15-minute job still writes message notices for the bell. `private.route_notification()` keeps\nthem off phones, so nobody gets a second alert for the same messages, and **never emails them**\n(G, 2026-11-09: `20270118120100_no_message_emails.sql`). The settings page offers only a Phone\nchoice for Messages.\n\nEmails are sent every 15 minutes by `.github/workflows/notifications.yml`, which runs\n`scripts/send-notifications.ts`. A Cloudflare timer starts it, because GitHub's own schedule can run\nhours late ([how it works](scheduling.md#starting-jobs-on-time)). Until the secrets below are set,\nnothing is emailed and notifications stay in the app. The secrets are set and the launch switch\nbelow is on, so **everyone gets email and alerts as they've chosen.**\n\n## Secrets (GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions)\n\n| Secret | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `SUPABASE_URL` | Already set for the keep-alive job |\n| `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` | Supabase → Project Settings → API keys → service role. Only ever an Actions secret: never in the app's runtime, the browser, the repo, or a log |\n| `RESEND_API_KEY` | Already set for failure alerts |\n| `NOTIFY_FROM` | The sender, for example `IFC Production Academy <academy@your-domain>`, on a domain verified in Resend |\n\nThe Academy's address for links in emails comes from the `SITE_URL` repository variable the\ndeploy workflow already uses.\n\n## What the job does\n\n1. Queues reminders (`queue_sop_review_reminders`, `queue_reminders`, `queue_serving_reminders`,\n   and `queue_cover_reminders`), each once:\n   - SOP review due a week before the review date, and overdue a week after it;\n   - \"Please confirm\" 48 hours after someone is scheduled, if they haven't answered;\n   - \"Serving soon\" from 10:00 two days before an event, to the confirmed volunteer, and to their\n     supervisor for the Academy's own services;\n   - \"Serving today\" from 08:00 on the day;\n   - \"Still to start\" 7 days after a course is assigned, if they haven't started it;\n   - \"Keep going\" 7 and 21 days after someone last worked on a course they started;\n   - a practical assessment request still open after 7 days, to the department's leaders;\n   - a competency expiring within 30 days, where expiry is on;\n   - a cover request still open within two days of the service, to those who schedule.\n\n   These cover Planning Center's services too, so some people also get Planning Center's own\n   reminders. G chose that, in `docs/decisions/2026-11-05-alerts.md`. Learning reminders (the nudges\n   and \"Still to start\") are limited to one a day per person, and a skipped one is written on a later\n   day. An assessment request also notifies the department's trainers the moment it's made.\n   Times follow decision 8 in `docs/decisions/2026-10-31-open-decisions.md`.\n2. Claims up to 50 pending emails at a time (`claim_email_notifications`), skipping kinds the\n   person turned off and accounts that aren't active.\n3. Sends each through Resend and records the result (`complete_email_notification`). A failed\n   send is retried on the next runs, up to three attempts.\n4. Sends onboarding pathway emails the same way (`claim_onboarding_emails`,\n   `complete_onboarding_email`), if onboarding emails are on. See below.\n\n**There are no quiet hours** (G, 2026-11-09, `20270118120200_no_quiet_hours.sql`). Emails and\nalerts go out at any time of day; until then they were held from 20:00 to 08:00 Winnipeg time.\n`private.alerts_held_now()` is where a hold would go back if it's ever wanted.\n\nThe job logs counts only: no addresses, names, or content. If every send in a run fails, the job\nfails and the failure alert emails the admins.\n\n## The launch switch\n\n**On since 2026-11-09** (`20270118120000_notifications_live.sql`), as the first volunteers start:\neveryone gets email and phone alerts as they've chosen. Decided in\n`docs/decisions/2026-11-09-notifications-live.md`.\n\nWhile `organizations.notifications_live` is false, notifications still show in the app, but aren't\nemailed or sent to devices. Each one is marked `skipped` as it's written, so **it's never sent\nlater**. What was held before 2026-11-09 stayed held.\n\nPeople in `notification_testers` are let through while it's off, so a pilot can check real emails.\nAdd them by account in the Supabase SQL editor, and never put their addresses in this repository:\n\n```sql\ninsert into public.notification_testers (user_id)\nselect id from auth.users where email = 'someone@example.com';\n```\n\nTo hold everything again (testers still get theirs):\n\n```sql\nupdate public.organizations set notifications_live = false;\n```\n\nThis switch doesn't affect sign-in links, or the job alerts and weekly summary that go to the\nadmins. Onboarding emails have their own switch, below.\n\n## Onboarding emails\n\nThese go to people on a pathway, who may not have an account yet. **On since 2026-11-09**\n(`20270118120200_no_quiet_hours.sql`): new emails are written to `onboarding_outbox` as `pending`\nand go out on the next run.\n\nWhile `organizations.onboarding_emails_enabled` is false, each email is written as `held` and\nnothing is sent. To hold them again, set it to false.\n\n- **Emails held before 2026-11-09 stay held.** They aren't sent when you switch on, so nobody gets a\n  welcome written for them weeks ago. Look through them and contact anyone who still needs one.\n- **Someone whose account is suspended or deactivated isn't emailed.** Their email is marked\n  `skipped`.\n- **People without an account are told to reply** if they'd rather not get these, so someone must\n  read the `NOTIFY_FROM` inbox.\n\n## Check it works\n\n- Run the workflow by hand (Actions → Notifications → Run workflow) and look for\n  `Notification emails: N sent, 0 failed`.\n- Do something that notifies you, wait for the next run, and check the email arrives with a link\n  back to the Academy.\n\n## Resend's limits\n\nThe free plan allows 100 emails a day and 3,000 a month, shared with sign-in links, and 2 requests a second. The sender waits between emails. When Resend says it's at its limit, the rest stay in the queue and go out on a later run, without using up a try. Any other failure gets 3 tries, and the job fails loudly so you're told.\n",
  "runbook-phone-test": "# Testing on real phones\n\nA 15-minute check to do on a real iPhone and a real Android phone before more volunteers join, and\nagain after a big change. The automated tests cover the screens, but only a real phone shows how\nsigning in, installing, and alerts feel. Do it with one person who knows the app and one who\ndoesn't.\n\nUse the live address. Tick each step, and if one doesn't work, use **Report a problem** in your\naccount menu straight away: it sends the page and phone with your note.\n\n## 1. Sign in (3 minutes)\n\n1. In Safari (iPhone) or Chrome (Android), open the Academy. You land on **Sign in**.\n2. Choose **Sign in with Planning Center** and sign in there. You come back to **Training**, signed in.\n3. Close the browser completely, open it again, and go back to the Academy. You're still signed in.\n\n## 2. Find your way around (3 minutes)\n\n1. Use each item on the bottom bar. Every page fits the screen: nothing slides sideways and no text is\n   cut off.\n2. Open your name (top right). Switch between dark and light. Both are easy to read.\n3. Open **Help** and read any guide. Pinch to zoom: the page lets you.\n\n## 3. A service checklist (4 minutes)\n\n1. Open **Serve**. The date tile shows the right day of the week, date, and time, matching Planning\n   Center.\n2. Tap the checklist button at the top. A sheet slides up with every checklist. Choose another one,\n   then your own again.\n3. Tick an item. The box ticks straight away. Wait 30 seconds and reload: it's still ticked.\n4. Tap **Still to do**. Ticked items hide and the count drops. Tap **All tasks**.\n5. Untick the item, so the real checklist is left as it was.\n\n## 4. Install it and get an alert (5 minutes)\n\n1. Open your name, then **Get the app**, and follow the steps for your phone.\n   - iPhone: Safari, **Share**, **Add to Home Screen**, **Add**.\n   - Android: Chrome, **Install app**.\n2. Open the Academy from the Home Screen icon. It opens full screen, without the browser bar, and\n   you're still signed in.\n3. Choose **Turn on alerts** and allow them. On iPhone this needs iOS 16.4 or later.\n4. Ask the other tester to send you a message in **Messages**. An alert drops down within a minute,\n   even with the app closed, and the icon shows a number until you read it.\n\n## What to write down\n\nFor anything that didn't work: the step number, the phone and its system version (Settings, About),\nand what you saw. **Report a problem** fills most of that in for you. Check **Admin → Problem\nreports** afterwards to see that both testers' notes arrived.\n",
  "runbook-planning-center-sign-in": "# Sign in with Planning Center\n\nThe production team signs in with their Planning Center account (checklist app US-01). Planning\nCenter governs sign-in; the email link stays behind \"Sign in with an email link\" for people who only\nwant training. A sign-in without Planning Center that has people choose their departments is\nplanned as the very last piece of work.\n\n## Who gets in\n\n- People in IFC's Planning Center as the sync sees it: on a production team's roster or on a\n  schedule. Someone just added in Planning Center can sign in after the next read (15 minutes, or\n  **Refresh now**).\n- While the Academy schedules (**Who schedules** on the Planning Center page), the sync no longer\n  reads teams, so anyone in IFC's Planning Center organization can sign in. They're recorded the\n  first time, with their Planning Center name. Team managers then add them to teams in the\n  Academy.\n- Only from IFC's Planning Center organization. The sync records its id.\n- The first time, the Academy creates their account quietly (no email) with their Planning Center\n  name and primary email, and links it to their Planning Center person. If an account already uses\n  one of their Planning Center emails, that account is linked instead. The link is permanent:\n  changing their email in Planning Center doesn't matter.\n- Suspended or deactivated accounts still can't use the Academy.\n\n## Setup (done 2026-10-31)\n\n1. In Planning Center: **api.planningcenteronline.com/oauth/applications → New application**.\n   Redirect URI, exactly:\n\n   ```text\n   https://ifc-production-academy.media-5f7.workers.dev/auth/planning-center/callback\n   ```\n\n2. Cloudflare secrets (paste each value when asked; never in a chat or a file):\n\n   ```sh\n   npx wrangler secret put PCO_CLIENT_ID\n   npx wrangler secret put PCO_CLIENT_SECRET\n   npx wrangler secret put SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY\n   ```\n\nUntil all three are set, the sign-in page shows the email link only.\n\n## How it works\n\n`/auth/planning-center` sends the person to Planning Center with a one-time state stored in an\nhttpOnly cookie. The callback checks the state, swaps the code for a token on the server, reads\n`/people/v2/me` (with emails) and the organization, and calls `planning_center_sign_in()` with the\nservice role. The token is used once and never stored or logged. The server then starts an\nordinary Academy session.\n",
  "runbook-push-alerts": "# Device alerts (web push)\n\nAlerts that drop down on phones and computers, like a native app's, for anyone who turns them on under **Notifications → Alerts on this device**. Decided by G on 2026-11-01: the Academy is installed as a Home Screen app (no app stores), and alerts arrive within seconds. Details are in `docs/decisions/2026-11-01-app-and-alerts.md`.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Something writes a notification (`private.notify`).\n2. A database trigger calls `POST /api/push/dispatch` through `pg_net`, sending the notification id and a token that the database generated itself (`private.app_settings`). The token never appears in the repo or a log.\n3. The app claims the notification once (`claim_push`), gets the person's devices, and sends each one an encrypted alert (`lib/domain/web-push.ts`, RFC 8291 and RFC 8292).\n4. Anything not sent within a minute is picked up by `notifications.yml` (every 15 minutes) through `claim_push_batch`. Alerts older than a day are dropped.\n5. A device the push service reports as gone (404 or 410) is deleted.\n6. Each alert carries the number for the app's icon: the person's unread notifications and messages together (`push_badges`, same token; `private.app_badge_count`). The service worker sets it with the Badging API. While the app is open, `components/shell/app-icon-badge.tsx` keeps it right (`my_app_badge`). Message notices aren't counted twice. If the lookup fails, the alert still goes, just without a number.\n\nThere are no quiet hours: alerts and emails go out at any time of day (G, 2026-11-09). Phones have their own Do Not Disturb.\n\n## Setup (once)\n\n1. Run `npm run push:keys` from the repo, signed in to the GitHub CLI. It makes a VAPID key pair (P-256) and saves:\n   - GitHub variable `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`: the public key (base64url, 65 bytes uncompressed).\n   - GitHub secret `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`: the private key (base64url `d`, 32 bytes). It's never printed or written to disk.\n2. Deploy (push to `main`, or run the Deploy workflow):\n   - The build gets the public key.\n   - The deploy copies the private key to the Worker with `wrangler secret put`.\n   - The deploy points the database at `<SITE_URL>/api/push/dispatch`.\n\nUntil both keys are set, alerts stay off and the Notifications page says so.\n\n## Rotating the keys\n\nNew keys invalidate every device's subscription. Rotate only if the private key leaks. After rotating (`npm run push:keys` again), everyone must turn alerts on again on each device.\n\n## iPhone and iPad\n\nAlerts work only after the Academy is added to the Home Screen and opened from there, on iOS or iPadOS 16.4 or later. The Notifications page explains this and shows the Share → Add to Home Screen steps.\n\n## Checking\n\n- Notifications page → **Send a test alert**: it goes straight to that device, with no notification written.\n- The `notifications.yml` log shows counts only: `Device alerts: N sent, N devices gone, N failed`.\n",
  "runbook-scheduling": "# Scheduling: Planning Center and the Academy\n\nPlanning Center Services schedules production. The Academy reads it every 15 minutes and shows it\nto volunteers with their checklists and training (decision record 9). Schedulers and volunteers\nkeep using Planning Center as they do now.\n\n## 1. Connect Planning Center (once)\n\nThe Academy uses the same read-only Personal Access Token as the Sunday Pre-Service Checklist app.\nIt needs **Services** and **People** (People is used only to read email addresses for matching).\n\nIn a terminal, in this repository, run each line and paste the value when asked. Never paste a\nsecret into a chat, an issue, or a file:\n\n```sh\ngh secret set PCO_PAT_ID\ngh secret set PCO_PAT_SECRET\n```\n\nThen run the job once by hand: **Actions → Planning Center sync → Run workflow**. Its log shows\ncounts only, such as \"plans 9, scheduled 31, people matched 18\". If it says Planning Center refused\nthe token, the token is wrong or doesn't include Services and People. If it says \"Couldn't read who\nschedules\", the Academy's own database refused the job: check `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` and that\nthe `planning_center_schedules()` migration is applied.\n\n### Starting jobs on time\n\nGitHub's own schedule can run hours late, so a Cloudflare timer on the Worker asks GitHub to run\nthe Planning Center sync and the notifications job every 15 minutes. The jobs and their secrets stay\non GitHub. GitHub's schedule stays on as a backup, so if the timer stops, the jobs still run, later.\n\n**Refresh now** on the Planning Center page runs the sync straight away instead\nof waiting up to 15 minutes. The button and the timer use the same token, which can only ask GitHub\nto run jobs. The Planning Center token stays in Actions secrets.\n\nTo set up that GitHub token:\n\n1. On GitHub: **Settings → Developer settings → Fine-grained personal access tokens → Generate\n   new token**. Resource owner: you. Repository access: **Only select repositories**, this one.\n   Permissions: **Actions: Read and write**, nothing else. Pick an expiry and note it.\n2. Add it to Cloudflare as a secret (paste when asked; never in a chat or file):\n\n   ```sh\n   npx wrangler secret put GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN\n   ```\n\nUntil it's set, the button doesn't show and the timer starts nothing. The button can be pressed once\nevery two minutes, and each press is in the audit log.\n\n**When the token expires,** the button says it couldn't reach GitHub, Sentry shows \"Could not start\nscheduled jobs\", and the jobs fall back to GitHub's slower schedule. Make a new token and put it\nagain. To check the timer is working, look under **Actions** for runs of these jobs marked\n`workflow_dispatch`, about every 15 minutes.\n\n## 2. Link positions\n\nIn the Academy, open **Admin → Planning Center**. After the first read it lists each Planning\nCenter service type, team, and position, like the checklist app's Team mapping.\n\n1. Create matching Academy positions first, in **Competencies and roles** (for example Camera 1,\n   Camera 2, Audio, ProPresenter, Production Director, Technical Director, Miscellaneous), each in\n   its department.\n2. On the Planning Center page, link each Planning Center position to its Academy position. A team\n   can also be linked as a whole; positions without their own link follow it.\n3. Links apply at the next read, within 15 minutes, or straight away with **Refresh now**.\n\n**Scheduled in positions not linked yet**, on the Position links tab, lists people on an upcoming service whose Planning\nCenter position isn't linked, or whose position name matches nothing in Planning Center. They\nwon't see a checklist until it's linked. Pre-service checklists shows the same warning for the\ncurrent service.\n\nThe **Not in the Academy** tab lists people on a Planning Center team or schedule with no Academy\naccount using one of their Planning Center emails. Invite them with that email.\n\n## 3. Set what each position sees on the day\n\nIn **Serving teams**, each position shows who is on it (from Planning Center's roster) and two\nswitches, as in the checklist app:\n\n- **Sees all departments:** whoever is scheduled here sees every checklist by default\n  (Technical Director).\n- **Gets the Director role while scheduled:** can reset and undo the service's checklists\n  (Technical Director, and Production Director if you like).\n\n## 4. Bring in the checklists\n\nIn **Pre-service checklists**, add one checklist per kind of position (Camera Operators, Audio\nEngineer, Presentation / Computer Graphics, Director (Switcher), Technical Director,\nMiscellaneous), each in its department, and link it to the positions that use it. Use **Paste a\nlist** to bring in the current checklist without retyping it: ready-to-paste text for all six\nlists is in the `private/checklists/` folder on G's computer, kept out of the public repository.\n\nThen, under **Pre-service checklists → Settings**, choose the Planning Center **service type** the\nchecklist follows (such as the Sunday service), so a youth night or rehearsal earlier in the week\ndoesn't open first, and the usual **service day** (Sunday). When nothing is published yet, the\nchecklist still opens for that day with a note saying so; once Planning Center publishes the\nservice, the next sync gives it that day's checklist, ticks included.\n\n## What volunteers see\n\n- **Your schedule:** where they're serving (from Planning Center), a month calendar, who's serving\n  with them, and their blockout dates. Accept, decline, and block out dates in Planning Center;\n  changes show within 15 minutes.\n- **Checklist:** today's service, or the next (or the next service day, if nothing is published\n  yet). Scheduled volunteers see their position's\n  checklist first; production team members who aren't serving can still help.\n\n## If IFC ever schedules in the Academy\n\nThe Academy's own scheduler (service types, the Matrix, sign-ups, and swaps) is built but switched\noff. An organisation-wide scheduler can switch it on under **Who schedules** on the Planning Center\npage. While Planning Center schedules, the Academy refuses schedule changes, service types\nincluded, from anyone but the sync, so the two can't drift apart.\n\nWhile the Academy schedules, the sync job checks the setting first and stops without reading\nPlanning Center. Its log says \"the Academy schedules production, so nothing was read\". The\ndatabase refuses the sync as well (`academy_schedules`), so nothing from Planning Center can\noverwrite the Academy's schedule. Switching to the Academy copies what's coming up: upcoming services keep\ntheir positions, who's scheduled and each reply, service types are made from Planning Center's,\nand team members and blockouts carry over, all keeping their Planning Center ids. Switching back\nhands those rows back, so the next run updates them instead of adding a second copy. Switching\nback starts the sync again at its next run. The plan for moving fully to the Academy is in the decision record\n`docs/decisions/2026-11-09-academy-scheduling.md`.\n\n## Service Dashboard run of service\n\nThe same job reads each plan's items, songs, item notes, and Services LIVE times from yesterday through the next 14 days, and applies them with `sync_service_plan_items()`. That's one extra request per plan, and it needs no new token or permission. It's read-only, like the rest. Past services keep the run of service they had.\n",
  "runbook-service-dashboard": "# Service Dashboard: setup\n\nFor whoever sets up the connections. New people should read [the guide](../guides/service-dashboard.md) instead.\n\nThe dashboard only reads. Nothing here can change a slide, the mixer, the encoder, or the stream.\n\n## YouTube live status and preview\n\nThe stream is public, so nobody signs in and nothing expires.\n\n1. **Channel ID:** in the Academy, go to Admin, Service Dashboard setup, Connections, and paste the channel ID. It starts with `UC`. The stream preview works from this alone.\n2. **API key, for the YouTube live tile:**\n   1. In [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), create a project, or use the backups project.\n   2. Enable **YouTube Data API v3**.\n   3. Under Credentials, create an **API key**. Restrict it to YouTube Data API v3.\n   4. Add it as a Cloudflare secret named `YOUTUBE_API_KEY` (Workers, the Academy, Settings, Variables and Secrets, as type Secret, or `wrangler secret put YOUTUBE_API_KEY`).\n\n**How it stays free:** it only looks from 30 minutes before a service until 30 minutes after. It searches for the stream every 2 minutes at most until it finds it, then checks every 30 seconds at most, however many screens are open. Google allows about 100 searches a day, and each check of a found stream costs far less.\n\n**Still to test on the real channel:** how soon after going live the search finds the stream.\n\nThe checks run while a screen is open during the service, so leave the wall TV on or a booth computer open.\n\n## Wall TV\n\n1. **Plug a small Android TV or Google TV stick into the TV's HDMI port.** A Roku TV can't run this by itself, because Roku has no proper web browser.\n2. Install a full-screen browser app on the stick, and open `<the Academy's address>/screen`. The TV shows a 6-digit code.\n3. On a phone or computer, sign in, go to Admin, Service Dashboard setup, Screens, and enter the code. This is for the editors and for whoever chooses them.\n4. Set the app to open that address when the stick starts.\n5. **On a Roku TV:** go to Settings, System, Power, Power on, and choose that HDMI input, so the TV returns to the dashboard after a power cut. The menu can differ by TV brand.\n\nThe TV holds a long-lived, view-only pass in a cookie, stored in the database only as a hash. To disconnect a TV, choose Remove beside it in Screens, and its pass stops working on its next refresh, within 30 seconds. If a TV's browser data is cleared, it shows a new code and needs adding again.\n\n**Still to test on the real stick:** that it reopens by itself after a power cut, and that the text can be read from where people stand.\n\n## The church bridge\n\nThe cloud can't reach the mixer, ProPresenter, or the Magewell, so a small program on a church computer reads them and sends the readings out. It only connects outward, so no ports open and no firewall changes are needed. It only reads: it never sends anything that can change a slide, the desk, or the encoder. It logs status and counts, never lyrics or names.\n\n**1. Make a secret, and give it to the Academy.** Make a long random secret (for example `openssl rand -hex 32`). Add it as a Cloudflare secret named `LIVE_ROOM_SECRET` (or `wrangler secret put LIVE_ROOM_SECRET`). Keep a copy for step 3. Without it the live tiles say \"Not connected yet\", and nothing else changes.\n\n**2. Put the program on a church computer** that stays on during services and is on the same network as the gear. It needs [Node.js 24](https://nodejs.org) and this repository, with no install step:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/gboyegaadeojo/IFC-Production-Academy.git\ncd IFC-Production-Academy\n```\n\n**3. Try it with example readings first,** which needs no gear:\n\n```bash\nACADEMY_URL=https://<the Academy's address> LIVE_ROOM_SECRET=<the secret> node bridge/main.ts --simulate\n```\n\nNode may print a warning about the module type. That is harmless.\n\nThe Slides, Timers, Levels, and Encoder tiles should fill with obviously fake readings. Stop it with Ctrl+C. A tile fades and says **Offline** about 10 seconds later.\n\n**4. Point it at the real gear.** Leave out any you don't have, and that tile says it's not reachable:\n\n| Setting | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `X32_HOST` | The X32's IP address (it uses UDP port 10023) |\n| `PROPRESENTER_URL` | `http://<its computer>:<port>`, with Network turned on in ProPresenter's preferences, where the port is shown |\n| `MAGEWELL_URL` | `http://<the encoder's address>` |\n| `MAGEWELL_USER`, `MAGEWELL_PASSWORD` | The encoder's sign-in, if it asks for one |\n\n**5. Start it with the computer,** using Task Scheduler on Windows or a systemd service on Linux, with those settings as environment variables. Don't put the secret in a file inside this repository.\n\n**To update it:** `git pull`, then restart it.\n\n**Free tier:** one message a second for a 4-hour Sunday counts as about 720 requests of the Durable Object's 100,000 a day, and about 1,800 of its 13,000 GB-s. Sending to screens is free. Slide pictures only go when the slide changes.\n\n### Still to test on the real gear\n\nThe bridge is built from each maker's documentation and tested with those documents' own examples, but it hasn't met the real gear yet. Check these first, using each tile:\n\n- **X32:** the levels move, and channel names appear. Each of the desk's meter sets is documented, but note that it allows only a few clients at once.\n- **ProPresenter:** the slide on screen and the next three are right, and pictures appear. Its published API and a client library disagree on one address, so the bridge tries both.\n- **Magewell:** whether it needs a sign-in, and that streams show correctly. Its `is-use` and `result` fields are what the bridge reads for \"Streaming\".\n\n### Another mixer\n\nLevels come from `bridge/x32.ts`. The Allen & Heath dLive has a documented connection, though its meter access is unconfirmed. We found no documented way to read meters on the Waves LV1 Classic. Another desk needs its own reader reporting the same channel ids (`lib/domain/mixer.ts`). Until then, leave `X32_HOST` out, and the other tiles carry on.\n"
};
